operator reads your terminal and can send you messages at any time; messages
sent while you work are queued and delivered at your next turn boundary.

Do not stop to ask questions you can resolve with a reasonable default: state
your assumption and keep going. Stop only when genuinely blocked.

Job dir: {jobdir}
Every ~30 minutes of work, append a timestamped line to PROGRESS.md in the job
dir (what you did, what's next). If you are genuinely blocked, do BOTH:
(1) append the question and the options you considered to QUESTIONS.md in the
job dir, (2) end your turn with a line starting `BLOCKED:`.
When the work is finished, write SUMMARY.md in the job dir and end your turn
with a line starting `DONE:`.

---
"""


# MSP transport preamble (issue #227): the same delegate conventions, but
# the agent is driven over the Muse Session Protocol instead of living in
# a tmux-managed TUI pane. No tmux- or pane-specific language.
PREAMBLE_MSP = """You are running as a delegate over the Muse Session Protocol. A human
operator reads your event stream and can steer you at any turn boundary.

Do not stop to ask questions you can resolve with a reasonable default: state
your assumption and keep going. Stop only when genuinely blocked.

Job dir: {jobdir}
Every ~30 minutes of work, append a timestamped line to PROGRESS.md in the job
dir (what you did, what's next). If you are genuinely blocked, do BOTH:
(1) append the question and the options you considered to QUESTIONS.md in the
job dir, (2) end your turn with a line starting `BLOCKED:`.
When the work is finished, write SUMMARY.md in the job dir and end your turn
with a line starting `DONE:`.

---
"""


def run(*argv, check=True, capture=True, timeout=120):
    p = subprocess.run(
        list(argv),
        stdout=subprocess.PIPE if capture else None,
        stderr=subprocess.PIPE if capture else None,
        timeout=timeout,
    )
    if check and p.returncode != 0:
        err = (p.stderr or b"").decode(errors="replace")[:500]
        raise RuntimeError(f"{' '.join(argv[:3])} failed: {err}")
    return p


def slug_ok(slug):
    return re.fullmatch(r"[a-z0-9][a-z0-9._-]{0,79}", slug) is not None


def repo_url_ok(url):
    # Git-clone option injection (issue #7): a leading dash makes git parse
    # the URL as flags (`--repo '--upload-pack=<cmd>'` would execute <cmd>
    # as the operator user; the operator routinely copies repo URLs from
    # issues/PRs/chat). Reject up-front; the clone call site additionally
    # passes `--` so the URL is never in option position even if a future
    # caller skips this check. Scheme whitelisting was considered and
    # rejected: local-path clones (`/home/.../repo`, `file://`) are
    # legitimate, and the dash+`--` pair closes the injection class.
    # Issue #793: `ext::`/`fd::` are remote-helper transports -- modern git
    # refuses ext:: by default, but an old-git box could still honor them
    # (spawning an external command as the operator). Reject the prefixes
    # up-front; no legitimate URL starts with either.
    return (
        isinstance(url, str)
        and bool(url)
        and not url.startswith("-")
        and not url.lower().startswith(("ext::", "fd::"))
    )


def job_dir(slug):
    return os.path.join(JOBS_DIR, slug)


def job_json_path(slug):
    return os.path.join(job_dir(slug), "job.json")


def trusted_job_paths(slug, job):
    """Paths the destructive commands (close, resume) may act on.

    job.json is agent-writable (issue #11): the job agent runs as the same
    user and can rewrite it wholesale. So:

    - `worktree` and `branch` are RE-DERIVED from the slug -- the derived
      layout (`~/muse-jobs/<slug>/work`, branch `job/<slug>`) is the
      canonical contract (see TOOL_INTERFACE.md). A recorded value that
      diverges from it fails closed with an explicit message: silent
      override would turn tampering (or an unsupported migration) into
      confident destruction of the wrong thing.
    - `pristine` cannot be re-derived, so it is containment-checked against
      `~/repos` AND bound to this job: the derived worktree must be a
      registered worktree of that repo (`git worktree list`). Containment
      alone would let a forged pristine point at any repo dir under
      `~/repos` and run `branch -D job/<slug>` there.

    Fail closed on any mismatch. Returns (worktree, pristine, branch).
    """
    jd = job_dir(slug)
    work = os.path.join(jd, "work")
    branch = f"job/{slug}"
    pristine = job.get("pristine")
    if not isinstance(pristine, str) or not pristine:
        raise RuntimeError("job has no recorded pristine repo; refusing destructive op")
    rp, rr = os.path.realpath(pristine), os.path.realpath(REPOS_DIR)
    if rp != rr and not rp.startswith(rr + os.sep):
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"recorded pristine repo {pristine!r} is outside {REPOS_DIR}; "
            "refusing destructive op (possible job.json tampering)")
    rec_work = job.get("worktree")
    if rec_work and os.path.realpath(rec_work) != os.path.realpath(work):
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"recorded worktree {rec_work!r} diverged from the derived "
            f"layout {work!r} -- possible job.json tampering or an "
            "unsupported migration; refusing destructive op")
    rec_branch = job.get("branch")
    if rec_branch and rec_branch != branch:
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"recorded branch {rec_branch!r} diverged from the derived "
            f"layout {branch!r} -- possible job.json tampering or an "
            "unsupported migration; refusing destructive op")
    try:
        out = run("git", "-C", pristine, "worktree", "list", "--porcelain",
                  timeout=30).stdout.decode()
    except Exception:
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"cannot list worktrees of pristine repo {pristine!r}; "
            "refusing destructive op")
    registered = {os.path.realpath(line.split(None, 1)[1])
                  for line in out.splitlines()
                  if line.startswith("worktree ") and len(line.split(None, 1)) == 2}
    if os.path.realpath(work) not in registered:
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"derived worktree {work!r} is not a registered worktree of "
            f"{pristine!r} -- possible job.json tampering (forged pristine); "
            "refusing destructive op")
    return work, pristine, branch


def load_job(slug):
    p = job_json_path(slug)
    if not os.path.exists(p):
        raise RuntimeError(f"unknown job: {slug}")
    with open(p) as f:
        d = json.load(f)
    # Pin the slug to the manager-side value: job.json is agent-writable
    # (issue #11) and every read-modify-write path (save_job, job_status,
    # verify_done_event's SUMMARY.md check) builds filesystem paths from the
    # slug. A recorded "slug": "victim" would overwrite another job's record;
    # "slug": "../../x" would write outside ~/muse-jobs. Every caller already
    # passes the trusted slug (argv / lock slug / listdir entry).
    d["slug"] = slug
    return d


def save_job(job, slug):
    # The write path comes from the MANAGER-side slug (argv / lock slug /
    # listdir entry), never job["slug"]: job.json is agent-writable
    # (issue #11), so a recorded "slug": "victim" / "../../x" must not
    # redirect the write. Callers pass their trusted slug; load_job also
    # pins the record's slug as defense in depth.
    p = job_json_path(slug)
    tmp = p + ".tmp"
    with open(tmp, "w") as f:
        json.dump(job, f, indent=2)
    os.replace(tmp, p)


def tmux_alive(slug):
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(
            ["tmux", "has-session", "-t", f"mjob-{slug}"],
            stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL,
            stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL,
        )
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return False  # tmux not installed: no session can be alive
    return p.returncode == 0


def _phase_concurrency_bars_resume(slug):
    """True when auto-resuming `slug` would break the one-phase-job-at-a-time rule.

    Issue #804 (user 2026-10-01): the standing rule is one live phase-* job
    on the box. watch's "one recovery attempt" used to resume every dead
    active job unconditionally -- resurrecting operator-killed phase-* jobs
    into four concurrent ultra-reasoning sessions on the user's pay-as-you-go
    bill. The operator's deployed copy carried this as a hot-patch
    (2026-10-01) until the loop shipped the repo version; the semantics here
    match the hot-patch verbatim: phase-* slugs only, barred while any other
    phase-* job has a live tmux session, OSError on the job-dir scan fails
    open (pre-hot-patch behavior).

    Check-then-act race (QA note): a sibling starting between this check
    and cmd_resume can transiently over-admit; the sibling-dying direction
    self-heals on the next pass. Narrow window, single-cron reality, and
    the hot-patch this matches had the same shape -- re-checking inside
    cmd_resume is follow-up material, not this fix.
    """
    if not slug.startswith("phase-"):
        return False
    try:
        siblings = sorted(os.listdir(JOBS_DIR))
    except OSError:
        return False
    return any(s != slug and s.startswith("phase-") and tmux_alive(s)
               for s in siblings if not s.startswith("."))


def with_lock(slug, fn):
    os.makedirs(JOBS_DIR, exist_ok=True)
    lockp = os.path.join(JOBS_DIR, f".{slug}.lock")
    with open(lockp, "w") as lf:
        fcntl.flock(lf, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
        try:
            return fn()
        finally:
            fcntl.flock(lf, fcntl.LOCK_UN)


def _host_of(authority):
    """Lowercased host from a URL authority, minus userinfo.

    An explicit port stays part of the key (`host:2222`): different ports
    on one host can serve different repositories, so dropping it would let
    distinct upstreams share one pristine clone (issue #10). Refuses
    fail-fast when the host is not a single path segment (issue #793).
    """
    auth = authority.split("@")[-1]
    if auth.startswith("["):
        host, _, rest = auth[1:].partition("]")  # IPv6 literal
        port = rest[1:] if rest.startswith(":") else ""
    else:
        host, _, port = auth.partition(":")
    if "/" in host:
        # The host must be a single path segment. A '/' here means the
        # input was not really "<host>:<path>" (e.g. a local path
        # containing ':'), and passing it through would break the
        # REPOS_DIR containment invariant (issue #793).
        raise RuntimeError(f"bad repo name from url: {authority!r}")
    host = (host or "unknown-host").lower()
    return f"{host}:{port}" if port else host


def pristine_name_from_url(url):
    """Pristine clone dir, relative to REPOS_DIR, for a repo URL (issue #10).

    Keys on host + full path, not the bare repo name: `org1/foo` and
    `org2/foo` previously shared one pristine clone, so the second
    spawn's `git fetch` pulled the wrong repo's objects into it and
    worktrees were cut from the wrong base. Returns e.g.
    `github.com/ntindle/spark-vm`; equivalent URLs normalize to one dir
    (`https://github.com/o/r`, `git@github.com:o/r.git`,
    `ssh://git@github.com/o/r` all land together -- an explicit port
    stays in the key, so `ssh://git@github.com:22/o/r` gets its own
    dir). Local paths and
    `file://` URLs namespace under `_local/<path...>`; a bare name keeps
    the old flat `<name>` shape.

    The host is part of the key: `github.com/org/foo` and
    `gitlab.com/org/foo` are different upstreams and must never share a
    clone. The full path is kept (no last-two-segment truncation), so
    GitLab subgroups (`host/a/sub/repo` vs `host/b/sub/repo`) stay
    distinct too. An explicit port stays in the key (`host:2222`) --
    different ports can serve different repos. Host case is folded (DNS
    is case-insensitive); path case is preserved for debuggability.
    `_local` is reserved for the local-path namespace: network hosts
    named `_local` are refused, and empty-path URLs (`https://host`,
    `git@host:`, `file://`) are refused rather than collapsing onto a
    bare-name dir.

    Containment: every returned component is a single URL path segment
    (or the derived host / `_local` marker), so no component can contain
    `/`. A `..`/`.` segment in any position is refused fail-fast before
    any filesystem work -- this extends issue #793's last-segment gate
    to every segment, and the derived path can never escape REPOS_DIR.
    """
    u = url.rstrip("/")
    if "://" in u:
        scheme, _, rest = u.partition("://")
        authority, _, path = rest.partition("/")
        if scheme.lower() == "file" or not authority:
            # file:// URLs and scheme-relative paths: no meaningful host.
            path_segs = [s for s in path.split("/") if s]
            if not path_segs:
                raise RuntimeError(f"bad repo name from url: {url!r}")
            segs = ["_local"] + path_segs
        else:
            host = _host_of(authority)
            if host == "_local" or host.startswith("_local:"):
                # `_local` is reserved for the local-path namespace below;
                # a network host with that name would share clone dirs
                # with local paths (issue #10).
                raise RuntimeError(f"bad repo name from url: {host!r}")
            path_segs = [s for s in path.split("/") if s]
            if not path_segs:
                # `https://host` (empty path) would derive the flat `<host>`
                # dir and collide with a bare-name `<host>` spawn (issue #10).
                raise RuntimeError(f"bad repo name from url: {url!r}")
            segs = [host] + path_segs
    elif ":" in u:
        # scp-like SSH syntax: [user@]host:path
        before, _, path = u.partition(":")
        if "/" in before:
            # Not scp-like at all (git reads `a/b:x` as a local path):
            # the "host" would smuggle `/` into one dir component and
            # collide with the https://a/b/x derivation (issue #10).
            raise RuntimeError(f"bad repo name from url: {before!r}")
        path_segs = [s for s in path.split("/") if s]
        if not path_segs:
            raise RuntimeError(f"bad repo name from url: {url!r}")
        host = _host_of(before)
        if host == "_local" or host.startswith("_local:"):
            # `_local` is reserved for the local-path namespace (issue #10).
            raise RuntimeError(f"bad repo name from url: {host!r}")
        segs = [host] + path_segs
    else:
        # Local path or bare name.
        rest = [s for s in u.split("/") if s]
        if not rest:
            return "repo"
        if rest == ["_local"]:
            # A real path literally named `_local` must not collapse onto
            # the namespace root or the empty-input fallback (issue #10).
            segs = ["_local", "_local"]
        else:
            segs = (["_local"] + rest) if len(rest) > 1 else rest
    if segs[-1].endswith(".git"):
        segs[-1] = segs[-1][:-4]
    segs = [s for s in segs if s]
    if not segs:
        return "repo"
    for seg in segs:
        if seg in ("..", "."):
            raise RuntimeError(f"bad repo name from url: {seg!r}")
    return os.path.join(*segs)


def cmd_spawn(args):
    slug = args.slug
    if not slug_ok(slug):
        raise RuntimeError(f"bad slug: {slug}")
    # Issue #7: reject git option-injection before the per-slug lock or any
    # filesystem work -- a leading-dash --repo would otherwise reach
    # `git clone` as flags.
    if not repo_url_ok(args.repo):
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"bad repo url: {args.repo!r} (rejected: "
            "git option injection)")

    def go():
        # Serialized on the per-slug lock (like cmd_steer): the exists
        # pre-check races without it -- a concurrent same-slug spawn during
        # the minutes-long clone would also pass the check, then fail
        # `worktree add -b` (branch already exists), and the except-cleanup
        # would destroy the WINNER's worktree, branch, and job dir.
        # Inside the lock the loser fails fast at the exists check.
        # MSP is the default transport (issue #228: muse-job v2); --tmux
        # opts back into the legacy TUI-pane path.
        # getattr: older callers/tests build the Namespace without --tmux.
        if getattr(args, "tmux", False):
            _spawn(slug, args)
        else:
            _spawn_msp(slug, args)

    with_lock(slug, go)
    return 0


def _spawn_prepare(slug, args, preamble, tmux_name):
    """Shared spawn prologue for both transports (tmux and MSP).

    Clone/fetch, worktree, secret-scanned prompt.md, job dir + job.json.
    Returns (job, work, jd); the caller launches the runtime (tmux pane or
    MSP session). On failure the job dir and git state are cleaned up, same
    as the old monolithic _spawn (BaseException so SIGTERM mid-clone also
    cleans up).
    """
    jd = job_dir(slug)
    if os.path.exists(jd):
        raise RuntimeError(f"job dir exists: {jd}")
    prompt_file = args.prompt_file
    if not os.path.exists(prompt_file):
        raise RuntimeError(f"prompt file missing: {prompt_file}")

    os.makedirs(REPOS_DIR, exist_ok=True)
    # Issue #10: the pristine clone is keyed on host+full-path, not the
    # bare last URL segment, so distinct upstreams never share one clone.
    # pristine_name_from_url fail-fasts on `..`/`.` in any segment
    # (issue #793's gate, extended to every segment); every derived
    # component is a single path segment and cannot escape REPOS_DIR.
    # Deliberately NOT slug_ok(): legitimate host/org/repo names may be
    # uppercase or longer than 80 chars; `..`/`.` are the only names that
    # escape the containment root, and every other segment stays inside it.
    pristine = os.path.join(REPOS_DIR, pristine_name_from_url(args.repo))
    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(pristine), exist_ok=True)
    base = args.base or "origin/HEAD"
    # Issue #793: --base reaches `rev-parse` in option position (hardening,
    # not a hole: rev-parse has no command-execution options). The `--`
    # separator is NOT available here -- rev-parse does not honor it before
    # the revision (post-`--` args are echoed verbatim, never resolved;
    # verified: `git rev-parse -- HEAD` prints a literal `--`) -- so the
    # gate is validation, checked before any clone/fetch work.
    if base.startswith("-"):
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"bad base ref: {base!r} (leading dash rejected: "
            "git option injection)")
    if not os.path.isdir(os.path.join(pristine, ".git")):
        print(f"cloning {args.repo} -> {pristine}", flush=True, file=sys.stderr)
        # Issue #7: `--` keeps the URL out of git's option position even if
        # a future caller bypasses repo_url_ok(); the leading-dash rejection
        # in cmd_spawn is the primary gate.
        run("git", "clone", "--", args.repo, pristine, timeout=600)
    else:
        run("git", "-C", pristine, "fetch", "origin", timeout=600)
    # resolve base to a sha for the record (base was validated above --
    # no re-derivation needed)
    sha = run("git", "-C", pristine, "rev-parse", base).stdout.decode().strip()

    work = os.path.join(jd, "work")
    os.makedirs(os.path.join(jd, "tmp"), exist_ok=True)
    try:
        run("git", "-C", pristine, "worktree", "add", work, "-b", f"job/{slug}", sha)

        with open(prompt_file) as f:
            prompt = f.read()
        # Issue #9: scan BEFORE persisting anything -- a refused prompt never
        # reaches prompt.md or the TUI. The PREAMBLE is ours and carries no
        # operator text, so only the user-supplied prompt is scanned.
        require_no_secrets(prompt, "spawn prompt", args.allow_secrets)
        full_prompt = preamble.format(jobdir=jd) + prompt
        with open(os.path.join(jd, "prompt.md"), "w") as f:
            f.write(full_prompt)
        for name in ("SUMMARY.md", "QUESTIONS.md", "PROGRESS.md"):
            open(os.path.join(jd, name), "a").close()

        job = {
            "slug": slug,
            "repo": args.repo,
            "pristine": pristine,
            "worktree": work,
            "branch": f"job/{slug}",
            "base": base,
            "base_sha": sha,
            "tmux": tmux_name,
            "session_uuid": None,
            "state": "active",
            "budget_hours": args.budget_hours,
            "started_at": time.time(),
            # Anchor for the events-missing liveness grace (issue #3): tracks the
            # CURRENT session, refreshed by the resume fallback when it discovers
            # a new session uuid. Falls back to started_at for legacy records.
            "session_started_at": None,
            "last_bytes": 0,
            "last_diffstat": "",
            "diffstat_strikes": 0,
            "pr_url": None,
        }
        save_job(job, slug)
    except BaseException:
        # Don't leave a job dir without a job.json: the next spawn with the
        # same slug would fail "job dir exists" until someone rm -rf's it by
        # hand, and a half-added `job/<slug>` branch would break the retry's
        # `worktree add -b` too. BaseException (not Exception) so SIGTERM /
        # KeyboardInterrupt mid-spawn -- the clone can run minutes -- also
        # cleans up. Best-effort git cleanup, then remove the dir.
        try:
            run("git", "-C", pristine, "worktree", "remove", "--force", work,
                check=False)
            run("git", "-C", pristine, "branch", "-D", f"job/{slug}",
                check=False)
        except Exception:
            # Cleanup must not mask the original failure (e.g. git missing
            # -> FileNotFoundError, or a hung git -> timeout).
            pass
        shutil.rmtree(jd, ignore_errors=True)
        raise
    return job, work, jd


def _spawn(slug, args):
    job, work, jd = _spawn_prepare(slug, args, PREAMBLE, f"mjob-{slug}")

    if tmux_alive(slug):
        run("tmux", "kill-session", "-t", f"mjob-{slug}", check=False)
    run("tmux", "new-session", "-d", "-s", f"mjob-{slug}", "-x", "220", "-y", "60",
        "-c", work)
    prompt_md = os.path.join(jd, "prompt.md")
    # Launch with the prompt as argv via shell $(cat): no TUI input racing,
    # no quoting hazards (command substitution results are not re-scanned).
    # Issue #699: the TUI's environment defers launcher auto-updates while
    # the job is active -- the job's own process tree never stages a mid-turn
    # update (see _TUI_NO_AUTO_UPDATE).
    launch = (
        f"cd {shquote(work)} && TMPDIR={shquote(os.path.join(jd, 'tmp'))} "
    )
    if shutil.which("sccache"):
        launch += "RUSTC_WRAPPER=sccache SCCACHE_CACHE_SIZE=20G "
    launch += _tui_launch_cmd("--yolo", f"\"$(cat {shquote(prompt_md)})\"")
    run("tmux", "send-keys", "-t", f"mjob-{slug}", launch, "Enter")
    # Issue #791: a fresh workdir is a workspace the TUI has never seen, so
    # the very first launch can block at the workspace-trust gate -- and
    # _spawn never answered it (only cmd_resume and the fallback-fresh path
    # did). The unanswered TUI session ends, the pane drops to a shell, and
    # the job strands until a human intervenes. Answer now, same posture as
    # the resume path: the workdir is slug-derived and operator-owned, so
    # "Trust and continue" grants nothing the job's agent doesn't already
    # have through its shell.
    if _answer_trust_prompt(slug):
        print("answered workspace-trust prompt", file=sys.stderr)
    # Discover the session uuid via the session index.
    uuid = None
    deadline = time.time() + 90
    while time.time() < deadline and uuid is None:
        uuid = find_session(work, job["started_at"])
        if uuid is None:
            time.sleep(5)
    if uuid is None:
        # stdout must stay a single JSON document (the client json.loads it);
        # progress/warnings go to stderr.
        print("WARNING: session uuid not discovered; steering still works via tmux",
              file=sys.stderr)
    else:
        job["session_uuid"] = uuid
        job["session_started_at"] = time.time()
        save_job(job, slug)
    print(json.dumps({"slug": slug, "session_uuid": uuid, "worktree": work}))
    return 0


def shquote(s):
    return "'" + s.replace("'", "'\\''") + "'"


def _is_main_session(rec):
    """Exclude internal children (e.g. reminder observers share the cwd but
    carry exactly one tool: submit_reminder_decision)."""
    try:
        tools = rec.get("tools")
        if tools is None:
            # records written before the payload trim (2026-09-17)
            tools = rec.get("payload", {}).get("tools") or []
        names = [t.get("name") for t in tools]
        return names != ["submit_reminder_decision"]
    except Exception:
        return True


def _muse_process_in_workdir(workdir):
    """True iff a muse-named process currently has `workdir` as its cwd.

    Issue #12 L5: find_session binds from the hook registry, which is
    same-user writable -- a planted record can name any session_id for any
    cwd. Corroborate the registry claim against the OS: a genuine discovery
    happens while the TUI we launched is running in this workdir. A
    candidate with no muse-named process in the workdir fails closed (the
    caller keeps polling / reports it unbound).

    Linux-only (/proc scan); elsewhere this is always False, so discovery
    degrades to the caller's poll-then-warn path. The name check is
    argv0-based (`muse` or `muse-bin-*`) -- trivially spoofable by any
    same-user process via `exec -a` -- so this is consistency enforcement,
    not authentication: a same-user writer that also runs a muse-named
    process in the workdir still passes.
    """
    if not os.path.isdir("/proc"):
        return False
    try:
        rwork = os.path.realpath(workdir)
    except Exception:
        return False
    for pid in os.listdir("/proc"):
        if not pid.isdigit():
            continue
        try:
            with open(os.path.join("/proc", pid, "cmdline"), "rb") as f:
                argv0 = f.read().split(b"\x00")[0].decode(errors="replace")
        except Exception:
            continue  # raced exit or unreadable: not our process
        name = os.path.basename(argv0)
        if name != "muse" and not name.startswith("muse-bin"):
            continue  # `muse` launcher and `muse-bin-*` binaries alike
        try:
            pcwd = os.path.realpath(os.path.join("/proc", pid, "cwd"))
        except Exception:
            continue
        if pcwd == rwork:
            return True
    return False


def find_session(workdir, since_ts, exclude=None):
    """Discover the session uuid via the muse-job hook registry.

    The plugin's PreLLMCall hook records {session_id, cwd, first_seen} for
    every session at its first model call -- far earlier and more reliably
    than the sqlite session index.

    `exclude` skips a known-stale session id (issue #3 review): the resume
    fallback must not re-adopt the dead session's registration, which a pure
    since_ts filter cannot reliably exclude within its 10s slack.
    """
    try:
        cands = []
        if not os.path.isdir(SESSIONS_DIR):
            return None
        rwork = os.path.realpath(workdir)
        for fn in os.listdir(SESSIONS_DIR):
            if not fn.endswith(".json"):
                continue
            try:
                with open(os.path.join(SESSIONS_DIR, fn)) as f:
                    rec = json.load(f)
            except Exception:
                continue
            if exclude is not None and rec.get("session_id") == exclude:
                continue
            rcwd = os.path.realpath(rec.get("cwd") or "")
            if rcwd == rwork and rec.get("first_seen", 0) >= since_ts - 10:
                cands.append((rec["first_seen"], rec["session_id"], rec))
        cands.sort()
        main = [c for c in cands if _is_main_session(c[2])]
        pool = main or cands
        # Newest first: the just-launched / just-resumed session is the one
        # we want. Oldest-first could adopt a stale dead registration --
        # notably on close+respawn of the same slug within the 10s slack,
        # where the old registration still matches.
        if not pool:
            return None
        # Issue #12 L5: the registry is same-user writable, so the
        # candidate is confirmed against the OS rather than trusted --
        # a genuine discovery happens while a live `muse` process runs in
        # this workdir. No such process -> fail closed (the caller's poll
        # loop keeps waiting). Residual, stated plainly: consistency
        # enforcement, not authentication.
        if not _muse_process_in_workdir(workdir):
            return None
        return pool[-1][1]
    except Exception:
        return None


def session_bound_to_workdir(uuid, workdir):
    """True iff the hook registry binds this session uuid to this workdir.

    `job["session_uuid"]` is agent-writable (issue #11), and `muse resume
    <uuid>` attaches the operator's tmux to whatever session the uuid names
    -- a forged uuid steers resume across the job boundary. Re-validate the
    binding at resume time and fail closed: the registry record's cwd must
    realpath-match the slug-derived workdir. This closes the direct job.json
    forgery (no matching record -> no resume). Residual, stated plainly: the
    registry itself is same-user-writable, so this is consistency
    enforcement -- planting a matching record takes a second, noisier write
    outside the job dir -- not authentication. (The done-claim triage in
    verify_done_event carries the same cooperative-telemetry caveat.)
    """
    try:
        rwork = os.path.realpath(workdir)
        if not os.path.isdir(SESSIONS_DIR):
            return False
        for fn in os.listdir(SESSIONS_DIR):
            if not fn.endswith(".json"):
                continue
            try:
                with open(os.path.join(SESSIONS_DIR, fn)) as f:
                    rec = json.load(f)
            except Exception:
                continue
            if (rec.get("session_id") == uuid
                    and os.path.realpath(rec.get("cwd") or "") == rwork):
                return True
        return False
    except Exception:
        return False


def _make_steer_needle(message):
    """Delivery-verification needle for steer arrival checks.

    Issue #12 L9: the old needle was the first 60 chars of the first
    line, so a stale agent echo of an earlier steer ("agent output
    echoing steer text can fake 'delivered'") could satisfy the arrival
    check for a new message sharing that prefix. The new needle is a
    (first-line, last-line) pair: the check matches the 60-char first-line
    prefix and the 60-char last-line suffix as substrings of the input-box
    region, so an echo only fools it when both affixes appear there.
    Returns ("", "") for blank input (no arrival check, as before).
    """
    stripped = message.strip()
    if not stripped:
        return ("", "")
    lines = stripped.splitlines()
    return (lines[0][:60], lines[-1][-60:])


def _input_box_has_text(pane, needle):
    """True if the TUI input box still holds the steer text.

    The box region is the last ❯-starting line plus its wrapped
    continuation rows. Wrap boundaries are display artifacts
    (capture-pane -p emits physical rows), so matching runs against the
    region with newlines removed: a needle part split across a wrap
    still counts as present. Echo protection is unaffected — the region
    is still anchored at the last ❯ line, so mid-transcript echoes sit
    outside it either way.
    """
    first, last = needle
    if not first and not last:
        return False
    lines = pane.splitlines()
    start = None
    for i, line in enumerate(lines):
        if line.strip().startswith("❯"):
            start = i
    if start is None:
        return False
    flat = "".join(lines[start:])
    return first in flat and last in flat


def _pane_shows_live_tui(pane, pane_cmd):
    """Fail-closed check: is the Muse TUI actually at its input prompt?

    `_steer` used to check only `tmux_alive()` -- never what is in the pane.
    If the TUI has exited or crashed, the pane drops to a bash prompt and a
    pasted steer message is parsed by the shell: `$(...)`, backticks, `;`,
    `|` all execute (issue #4). Two independent signals are required:

    1. Process: the pane's foreground process must be the TUI itself
       (`_is_tui_process`). Content alone is spoofable -- Starship's default
       prompt character is `❯`, so a dead pane with such a prompt passes
       signal 2 deterministically. Process identity is not spoofable by
       pane output.
    2. Content: a TUI input-box line (`❯`) is visible in the viewport tail,
       and none of the TUI's blocking states (resume banner, trust prompt)
       is showing there. Everything below the input box must be TUI chrome
       (divider rule / model footer): a shell prompt under a stale `❯` line
       means the TUI died and the pane dropped to a shell (issue #4).

    The input box is NOT required to be the viewport's last line: in the
    TUI's idle state the model footer renders below it. Blocking states are
    matched on the tail only -- a stale banner scrolled into the upper
    viewport must not misread a recovered TUI as still dead.

    Anything else (dead TUI, shell prompt, capture failure) refuses.
    """
    if not pane or not _is_tui_process(pane_cmd):
        return False
    tail = _pane_tail(pane).splitlines()
    if any(_RESUME_BANNER_TEXT in line for line in tail):
        return False
    # Issue #961: the trust-gate veto requires the gate's full observed
    # shape (question + option line, _pane_shows_trust_gate), not the bare
    # question substring -- a live TUI discussing the gate in its own
    # conversation must keep reading as live.
    if _pane_shows_trust_gate(pane):
        return False
    box = None
    for i, line in enumerate(tail):
        if line.strip().startswith("❯"):
            box = i
    if box is None:
        return False
    # A dead banner screen whose input box holds our own unsubmitted
    # recovery command (the TUI swallowed the Enter) is not a live TUI:
    # the banner hint line is replaced by our typed text, so the banner
    # check above already missed it. Classifying this as live would
    # silence the watchdog on a dead pane and let a later _steer paste
    # agent text into the dead box. Fail closed: the watchdog emits a
    # loud tui-dead every pass instead.
    if _RESUME_BANNER_CMD in tail[box]:
        return False
    return all(_tui_chrome_line(l.strip()) for l in tail[box + 1:])


def _wait_live_tui(slug, timeout=30):
    """Poll until the pane shows a live TUI or the timeout expires.

    Returns the last captured (pane, pane_cmd); check it with
    `_pane_shows_live_tui`. ("", "") means capture itself failed.
    The poll absorbs the normal TUI boot window after spawn/resume so the
    fail-closed refusal only fires when the TUI genuinely is not coming up.
    """
    target = f"mjob-{slug}"
    deadline = time.time() + timeout
    state = ("", "")
    while True:
        state = _capture_pane_state(target)
        if _pane_shows_live_tui(*state):
            return state
        if time.time() >= deadline:
            return state
        time.sleep(3)


# Foreground process names that count as the live Muse TUI in tmux
# `pane_current_command`. Anything else (bash/sh/zsh/...) means the pane is
# a shell -- even if its last line starts with `❯` (issue #4: Starship's
# default prompt character IS `❯`, and any PS1 can start with it, so the
# content heuristic alone is spoofable by a dead pane's own prompt).
_TUI_COMMANDS = frozenset({"muse", "node"})


def _is_tui_process(pane_cmd):
    """True when the pane's foreground process is the Muse TUI itself.

    Version-tolerant: the TUI binary presents as `muse-bin-<version>`
    (observed muse-bin-1.3.0-R3233.1 and muse-bin-1.3.0-R3401.1) as well as
    the plain `muse` launcher and `node`. Anything else (bash/sh/zsh/...)
    means the pane is a shell -- even if its last line starts with `❯`
    (issue #4). tmux reports the real foreground process name; pane output
    cannot fake it, so widening the name set does not weaken the
    anti-spoofing property: the bar is "the fg process really is the TUI
    binary", not "the name is on a short list".
    """
    return pane_cmd in _TUI_COMMANDS or pane_cmd.startswith("muse-bin-")


# Viewport tail examined for the TUI's current-state UI (input box, resume
# banner, trust prompt). These elements always render near the bottom; the
# upper viewport can hold stale copies (e.g. the banner text remains visible
# above after a successful /resume), so whole-pane matching would misread a
# recovered TUI as still dead.
_VIEWPORT_TAIL_LINES = 12


def _pane_tail(pane, n=_VIEWPORT_TAIL_LINES):
    lines = pane.splitlines()
    return "\n".join(lines[-n:])


def _tui_chrome_line(s):
    """Non-content lines the TUI renders below its input box: the divider
    rule and the model footer (`name · plan · <cwd> · YOLO`). Anything else
    below the input box -- e.g. a shell prompt under a stale `❯` line --
    means the TUI is not at its input prompt (issue #4)."""
    return not s or set(s) <= {"─", " "} or "·" in s


# Substring of the TUI's own resume hint. If a future TUI version rewords
# the banner, detection degrades to `tui-dead` (loud, manual remedy) --
# fail-loud, never silent.
_RESUME_BANNER_TEXT = "/resume reopens a past session"
# Exact hint line rendered on the dead-session banner. Recovery is only
# authorized on this exact line -- see _pane_shows_resume_banner.
_RESUME_BANNER_LINE = "❯ /resume reopens a past session (--last for the latest)"

# Substring of the TUI's workspace-trust gate ("Do you trust this
# workspace? ... 1 Trust and continue"). Observed blocking `muse resume`
# unattended 2026-09-21.
_TRUST_PROMPT_TEXT = "Do you trust this workspace?"
# The gate's option line. Issue #961: matching the question substring alone
# misfires on a live TUI whose own conversation happens to contain the
# phrase -- the gate classifier then reads live input as a gate and the
# auto-answer types "1"+Enter into a working session's input box. Requiring
# the option line too restores the two-signal shape: a mere mention of the
# question no longer matches.
# Issue #993: the bare option-text substring above still misfires on a
# live TUI whose own conversation mentions the option as prose --
#   The TUI asked 'Do you trust this workspace?'
#   I chose 'Trust and continue'
# carries both substrings on adjacent lines with NO selector marker, so
# the two-signal shape matches and the auto-answer fires into a working
# session. The real gate renders its options as a selector list
# (`> 1  Trust and continue`), so match the selector marker instead of
# the bare text: the misfire prose never carries `> N`, while the live
# gate always does. A verbatim pasted gate transcript still matches
# (text-only by design, issue #4). If the TUI rewords or renumbers the
# marker the match fails and the job degrades to tui-dead (loud, manual
# remedy) -- fail-loud, never silent.
_TRUST_OPTION_MARKER_RE = re.compile(r">\s*\d+\s*Trust and continue")
# Issue #972: the two halves of the gate must sit on SEPARATE lines with the
# option line after the question line, within a small window. The real gate
# renders them ~2 lines apart (question, `Workspace:` line,
# `> 1  Trust and continue`). The #961 tail-substring check is
# order-insensitive and position-insensitive: a single line like
# `I chose "Trust and continue" when it asked "Do you trust this workspace?"`
# in a live TUI's own conversation still misfires. This window restores the
# positional half of the gate shape.
_TRUST_GATE_LINE_WINDOW = 3


def _pane_shows_trust_gate(pane):
    """True when the viewport tail shows the TUI's workspace-trust gate.

    Both halves of the gate's observed shape must appear on separate lines:
    the question line, then the option line after it within
    _TRUST_GATE_LINE_WINDOW lines. Issue #961: a bare question-substring
    match fires when a live TUI's conversation merely mentions the phrase
    (e.g. the agent discussing the gate), which the watch loop then answers
    into a live input box. Issue #972: the #961 tail-substring check is
    order-insensitive and position-insensitive, so a single line containing
    BOTH phrases (e.g. the agent pasting a gate transcript, or `I chose
    "Trust and continue" when it asked "Do you trust this workspace?"`)
    still misfires. Process identity is NOT checked here -- the call
    sites keep their own _is_tui_process gates (a stale gate in a dead
    pane's scrollback is still text-only evidence, issue #4).
    """
    lines = _pane_tail(pane).splitlines()
    for i, line in enumerate(lines):
        if _TRUST_PROMPT_TEXT not in line:
            continue
        # The option half must follow on its own line: same-line co-occurrence
        # (a transcript paste, a one-line summary) is a misfire, not a gate.
        for j in range(i + 1, min(i + 1 + _TRUST_GATE_LINE_WINDOW, len(lines))):
            if _TRUST_OPTION_MARKER_RE.search(lines[j]):
                return True
    return False


def _capture_pane_state(target):
    """(pane text, foreground process name) for the target's active pane.

    Fail closed: any capture failure yields ("", ""), which never passes
    `_pane_shows_live_tui`.
    """
    try:
        pane = run("tmux", "capture-pane", "-p", "-t", target).stdout.decode(
            errors="replace")
    except Exception:
        pane = ""
    try:
        cmd = run("tmux", "display-message", "-p", "-t", target,
                  "#{pane_current_command}").stdout.decode(
            errors="replace").strip()
    except Exception:
        cmd = ""
    return pane, cmd


def _dead_tui_msg(target, pane):
    """Refusal error for a pane that does not show a live TUI (issue #4)."""
    # The pane tail is agent-influenced; sanitize like every other
    # operator-facing surface (#12 L3 class) before it reaches stderr.
    tail = _clean_text("\n".join(pane.splitlines()[-8:])) \
        if pane else "<no pane captured>"
    return (f"pane of {target} does not show a live TUI input box -- refusing "
            f"to steer (issue #4; pane tail:\n{tail})")


# --- dead-TUI detection and gentle recovery ---
# A Muse session can die from underneath the TUI (observed: "model failed:
# model stream idle timeout after 180000ms"): no Stop/SessionEnd hook fires,
# the tmux session stays up, and the pane parks at the TUI's session-picker
# banner ("/resume reopens a past session ..."). Hook-event liveness then
# stays silent indefinitely while `status` reports session=healthy from
# stale HEAD.json. The watchdog detects this state and attempts the gentle
# recovery the banner itself advertises (`/resume --last`), which reopens
# the SAME session uuid in place -- no kill, no new session, the event
# stream continues.

# Fixed literal sent to a resume-banner pane. Hardcoded, never
# agent/user-influenced: even pasted into a misclassified pane it is a
# command-not-found no-op -- no shell metacharacters, no interpolation.
_RESUME_BANNER_CMD = "/resume --last"


def _pane_shows_resume_banner(pane, pane_cmd):
    """True when the pane is the TUI's dead-session banner, not a live TUI.

    Two independent signals, same shape as _pane_shows_live_tui:
    1. Process: the foreground process is still the muse binary. A dead pane
       that dropped to a shell is NOT this -- never paste there (issue #4).
    2. Content: the TUI's own resume hint sits on the input-box line in the
       viewport tail.

    The hint must match EXACTLY (`_RESUME_BANNER_LINE`): the recovery this
    authorizes reopens the session, so a looser substring could fire on
    agent-typed text sitting in a live input box.
    """
    if not pane or not _is_tui_process(pane_cmd):
        return False
    return any(line.strip() == _RESUME_BANNER_LINE
               for line in _pane_tail(pane).splitlines())


def _pane_banner_cleared(pane, pane_cmd):
    """True when the resume banner is gone from the tail and a TUI input box
    is showing there.

    Recovery-poll predicate only: it decides a boolean (recovered or not),
    never authorizes pasting agent text. Both signals are required -- the
    process signal matters here too:

    - A pane that dropped to a shell after a failed /resume carries a `❯`
      prompt line (issue #4): content alone would read it as cleared, and
      the watchdog would log a false `tui-recovered`.
    - An unsubmitted echo of our own recovery command in the input box (the
      TUI swallowed the Enter) is not a cleared banner: the banner screen
      is still up and the hint line is replaced by our typed text.

    A loose True here cannot cause an unsafe steer (_steer re-checks
    _pane_shows_live_tui strictly before any paste), but it WOULD make the
    watchdog claim a dead pane is live again -- the exact silent masquerade
    this file exists to kill.
    """
    if not pane or not _is_tui_process(pane_cmd):
        return False
    tail = _pane_tail(pane).splitlines()
    if any(_RESUME_BANNER_TEXT in line for line in tail):
        return False
    if any(_RESUME_BANNER_CMD in line for line in tail):
        return False
    return any(line.strip().startswith("❯") for line in tail)


def _recover_resume_banner(slug, timeout=60):
    """Send the banner's own recovery command and wait for a live TUI.

    Uses raw tmux send-keys (never _steer: _steer refuses dead panes by
    design, and this pane is definitionally dead). Text and Enter are
    separate send-keys calls, per the project's tmux input rule. Returns
    True only when the banner is gone AND the pane passes the same
    two-signal bar as _pane_shows_live_tui (foreground process is the TUI,
    input box showing, no unsubmitted recovery echo): anything less would
    let the watchdog log `tui-recovered` for a shell pane (issue #4) or a
    dead banner screen.
    """
    target = f"mjob-{slug}"
    run("tmux", "send-keys", "-t", target, "-l", _RESUME_BANNER_CMD)
    time.sleep(2)
    run("tmux", "send-keys", "-t", target, "Enter")
    deadline = time.time() + timeout
    while True:
        pane, cmd = _capture_pane_state(target)
        if _pane_banner_cleared(pane, cmd):
            return True
        if time.time() >= deadline:
            return False
        time.sleep(5)


def _tui_swap_event(job, pane_cmd):
    """Detect an in-place Muse TUI auto-update under a running job (#212).

    The TUI's update prompt/notice states cannot be matched on text (the
    prompt wording is not grounded anywhere), but the update EVENT is
    observable: the pane's foreground process name is the versioned TUI
    binary (`muse-bin-1.3.0-R3233.1`, `-R3401.1`, ...) and an auto-update
    that swaps the binary shows up as a *change* in that name across
    watchdog passes while tmux stays alive.

    Returns the `tui-updated` event dict (without the `job` key) on a
    confirmed swap, else None. Maintains the `tui_cmd` baseline in `job`
    (manager-owned watchdog state, same class as `last_bytes`); the caller
    persists the job afterwards.

    Baseline rules:
    - Absent or unusable baseline (not a TUI process name): adopt the
      current name silently -- a pre-#212 job has no baseline, and a
      poisoned value is a baseline repair, not a pageable anomaly. Never
      adopt a non-TUI name (a shell at adoption time would fire a bogus
      swap the moment the TUI relaunches).
    - Current name not a TUI process (shell, capture failure): not a
      swap -- the dead-TUI path owns that case (`tui-dead`, loud). The
      baseline is kept so a later relaunch with a new binary still fires.
    - Both TUI process names and different: the swap. The event is
      diagnostic, not a recovery action: the per-pass liveness probe
      (tui-recovered / tui-unrecovered / tui-dead) already ran this pass
      via job_status, and the policy half of #212 (auto-accept vs defer
      updates while a job is active) is deliberately undecided here --
      detection only, no keystrokes sent.

    Trust note (#23 class): `tui_cmd` lives in agent-writable job.json, so
    a same-user agent can plant it to suppress one informational event.
    That suppresses only the diagnosis -- the fail-loud liveness signals
    (tui-dead every pass on a parked pane) do not depend on the baseline.

    One acknowledged limitation: a launcher<->versioned-binary presentation
    flip (e.g. `muse` -> `muse-bin-...`) with no real update also fires.
    The bar is "the foreground process really changed identity", which is
    still worth logging -- the event is diagnostic, never a recovery
    trigger.
    """
    if not isinstance(pane_cmd, str) or not _is_tui_process(pane_cmd):
        return None
    old = job.get("tui_cmd")
    if not isinstance(old, str) or not _is_tui_process(old):
        # No baseline (or a poisoned one): adopt silently, no event.
        job["tui_cmd"] = pane_cmd
        return None
    if old == pane_cmd:
        return None
    job["tui_cmd"] = pane_cmd
    return {"signal": "tui-updated",
            "detail": f"TUI binary changed under the running job "
                      f"({_clean_text(old)} -> {_clean_text(pane_cmd)}); "
                      f"the pane liveness probe ran this pass -- see the "
                      f"tui-recovered / tui-dead / tui-unrecovered signals "
                      f"for the post-swap state (issue #212; detection only, "
                      f"no update-accept policy applied)"}


def _emit_tui_swap_event(job, slug, pane_cmd, events):
    """One watchdog pass of the TUI-binary-swap check (issue #212).

    Extracted from cmd_watch so the emission contract is unit-testable:
    appends the `tui-updated` event (with the `job` key) to `events` on a
    confirmed swap and always persists the (possibly adopted/advanced)
    baseline via save_job. Callers hold the per-slug lock.
    """
    swap = _tui_swap_event(job, pane_cmd)
    save_job(job, slug)
    if swap is not None:
        swap["job"] = slug
        events.append(swap)


def _steer(slug, message, verify=True):
    """The one place the tmux input quirk lives.

    The message is written to a temp file, loaded into a tmux buffer, and
    pasted -- never sent via send-keys text. tmux send-keys translates
    embedded newlines into Enter, so a multi-line message sent as text would
    be submitted as several separate messages (the first line submits
    immediately). Enter is always a SEPARATE send-keys call after a pause:
    never combine text+Enter in one call, which silently no-ops against the
    TUI input box.
    """
    target = f"mjob-{slug}"
    if not tmux_alive(slug):
        raise RuntimeError(f"tmux session {target} not alive")
    # Issue #4: a live tmux session is not a live TUI. If the TUI exited or
    # crashed, the pane is a shell prompt and the paste below becomes shell
    # execution. Refuse to send at all -- never steer into a dead pane.
    pane, pane_cmd = _wait_live_tui(slug, timeout=30)
    if not _pane_shows_live_tui(pane, pane_cmd):
        raise RuntimeError(_dead_tui_msg(target, pane))
    # TOCTOU: re-check on a FRESH capture immediately before pasting -- the
    # poll above leaves a window where the TUI can die before the text lands.
    # (The check lives before tmp-file creation so the refusal path leaves
    # no steer text on disk either.)
    # Issue #321: the refusal reports the FRESH pane, not the stale
    # pre-TOCTOU capture -- the reported tail must match the refusal
    # instant, not what the pane showed during the earlier poll.
    fresh_pane, fresh_cmd = _capture_pane_state(target)
    if not _pane_shows_live_tui(fresh_pane, fresh_cmd):
        raise RuntimeError(_dead_tui_msg(target, fresh_pane))
    tmpd = os.path.join(job_dir(slug), "tmp")
    os.makedirs(tmpd, exist_ok=True)
    tmpf = os.path.join(tmpd, f"steer-{int(time.time() * 1000)}.txt")
    with open(tmpf, "w") as f:
        f.write(message)
    buf = f"mjob-steer-{slug}"
    try:
        run("tmux", "load-buffer", "-b", buf, tmpf)
        run("tmux", "paste-buffer", "-b", buf, "-t", target)
    finally:
        run("tmux", "delete-buffer", "-b", buf, check=False)
        try:
            os.remove(tmpf)
        except OSError:
            pass
    time.sleep(2)
    # TOCTOU: the TUI may have died between the paste and this Enter -- a
    # dead pane would execute the pasted text (multi-line text can even
    # execute at paste time, newline by newline). Do NOT send Enter into a
    # dead pane: pasted-but-unsubmitted text is inert. Report failure.
    pane, pane_cmd = _capture_pane_state(target)
    if not _pane_shows_live_tui(pane, pane_cmd):
        return False
    needle = _make_steer_needle(message)
    if verify and any(needle) and not _input_box_has_text(pane, needle):
        # Issue #12 L9 -- arrival check. The needle is the message's
        # first-line prefix + last-line suffix over the input-box region
        # (last ❯ line + wrapped continuation rows), so a stale agent echo
        # of the steer text can no longer fake arrival: the echo would
        # have to reproduce the first AND last lines verbatim *in the
        # box*. The clearance check below reports "delivered" when the
        # box no longer holds the steer needle, but a box the paste never
        # reached is equally clear of it: a silently failed paste (or
        # agent output echoing the steer text into the pane) would read
        # as a delivery that never happened. Fail closed: the needle must
        # be seen in the box at least once before the box going clear can
        # count as delivered.
        time.sleep(2)
        pane, pane_cmd = _capture_pane_state(target)
        if (not _pane_shows_live_tui(pane, pane_cmd)
                or not _input_box_has_text(pane, needle)):
            return False
    run("tmux", "send-keys", "-t", target, "Enter")
    if not verify:
        return True
    # The TUI can swallow Enters while it settles (observed after a resume:
    # the paste rendered in the box but several Enters never submitted).
    # Be patient here rather than declaring failure fast: the loop only
    # re-sends Enter while our text is still sitting in the box, so once it
    # submits, the box clears and no stray empty Enters go out.
    time.sleep(8)
    for _ in range(5):
        pane, pane_cmd = _capture_pane_state(target)
        # Issue #4, TOCTOU half: the TUI may have died between the pre-send
        # check and the Enter. A dead pane swallows the pasted text into a
        # shell -- the old "box is clear" test would then wrongly report
        # success (the text is gone because it executed). The TUI must still
        # be up for the steer to count as delivered.
        if not _pane_shows_live_tui(pane, pane_cmd):
            return False
        # Submitted (or queued mid-turn): the input box is clear of our text.
        if not _input_box_has_text(pane, needle):
            return True
        # Still sitting in the box (e.g. the paste rendered without
        # committing): try Enter once more.
        run("tmux", "send-keys", "-t", target, "Enter")
        time.sleep(10)
    return False


def cmd_steer(args):
    # Issue #9: the steer message is the second operator->agent funnel.
    # Refuse before the per-slug lock is even taken -- no partial state
    # (no steer tmp file, no TUI paste) exists on the refusal path.
    require_no_secrets(args.message, "steer message", args.allow_secrets)
    job = load_job(args.slug)
    if _msp_transport(job):
        return _msp_steer(job, args)

    def go():
        ok = _steer(args.slug, args.message)
        print(json.dumps({"slug": args.slug, "delivered": ok}))
        if not ok:
            raise RuntimeError("input not delivered after 3 tries")
    with_lock(args.slug, go)
    return 0


def maybe_adopt_session(job, slug):
    """Late session-uuid adoption for jobs whose spawn-time discovery missed.

    Returns (uuid_or_None, attention_detail_or_None). Fails closed: `started_at`
    is agent-writable (issue #11), and a garbage value would make find_session
    search from epoch zero -- adopting the oldest-ever registration, possibly
    a dead session, and blinding liveness/done-claim/tamper monitoring to the
    real session permanently (adoption is one-shot). Refuse loudly instead.
    """
    work = os.path.join(job_dir(slug), "work")
    ts = _num(job.get("started_at"), 0)
    if ts <= 0:
        return None, ("job started_at is invalid; refusing late session uuid "
                      "adoption (possible job.json tampering)")
    found = find_session(work, ts)
    return (found, None) if found else (None, None)


# Session ids double as event filenames; keep the manager-side shape check
# identical to the hooks' whitelist so a traversal-shaped or garbage uuid
# can never redirect a path join (final security review). The hooks refuse
# to write for a bad sid; the manager refuses to READ for one and the
# caller pages events-tampered loudly (a planted dict/list/"../../x" must
# not silently blind liveness and done triage).
_SID_RE = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,128}$")


def _session_uuid_ok(job):
    """Return (uuid, malformed) for the job's recorded session uuid.

    Genuinely absent (None/"") is (None, False): silence during legitimate
    initial discovery. Present-but-malformed -- non-string, overlong,
    or outside [A-Za-z0-9_-] -- is (None, True): the caller must page
    events-tampered rather than silently skipping the job's event stream.
    """
    uuid = job.get("session_uuid")
    if uuid is None or uuid == "":
        return None, False
    if isinstance(uuid, str) and _SID_RE.match(uuid):
        return uuid, False
    return None, True


def _event_path(uuid):
    """Event-file path for a hook-shaped uuid, else None (never join a
    hostile or garbage uuid into a filesystem path)."""
    if not isinstance(uuid, str) or not _SID_RE.match(uuid):
        return None
    return os.path.join(EVENTS_DIR, uuid + ".jsonl")


def head_info(uuid):
    if not isinstance(uuid, str) or not _SID_RE.match(uuid):
        return None, 0
    p = os.path.join(VIEW_DIR, uuid, "HEAD.json")
    try:
        with open(p) as f:
            d = json.load(f)
        return d.get("status"), d.get("source_total_bytes", 0)
    except Exception:
        return None, 0


# Issue #23 H6: the event-file scan is capped to the last 2 MB. A full-file
# readlines() per job per watch pass is a RAM/CPU DoS on the manager against
# a GB-scale appended file. Honest tradeoff (final security + arch review):
# a genuine event pushed past the tail window is INVISIBLE to the scan, and
# no in-repo mechanism recovers it -- so truncation is a per-pass LOUD
# signal (events-scan-truncated), never a silent condition. The 2 MB cap is
# containment, not correctness; the real fix is manager-owned rotation at
# 2x the window (issue #27, rotate_event_file below), which archives the
# oversized file and re-seeds its terminal done claims into the fresh file
# so a rotated-out genuine done keeps paging instead of becoming a silent
# burial. The 30-day disk sweep prunes rotated archives by mtime (they never
# match an active uuid's skip name), so rotation and the sweep compose.
_EVENT_SCAN_MAX_BYTES = 2 * 1024 * 1024

# Discovery-grace window for the liveness signals (issue #23): an absent
# session_uuid (or a stale event stream) is legitimate only this long after
# the session started. Anchored to the tmux session creation time (server
# side) via _grace_anchor, with _session_anchor as fallback.
_DISCOVERY_GRACE_S = 30 * 60


def _iter_events(uuid, max_bytes=_EVENT_SCAN_MAX_BYTES):
    """Yield parseable event dicts from the session's event file, oldest first.

    Refuses non-regular event files without opening them (issue #3, final
    review): lstat + S_ISREG -- a planted symlink, FIFO, device, or
    directory is tampering, not an event stream. A blocking open(2) on a
    planted FIFO used to hang the whole watch pass; now the caller pages
    `events-tampered` and the read side never blocks. Only dicts are
    yielded (issue #23 B4): a forged non-dict JSON line (`[1,2]`, `"x"`)
    would otherwise reach `ev.get("state")` and raise AttributeError,
    blinding that job's watch pass permanently (the line persists until the
    30-day prune; cmd_status crashes the same way). The docstring promises
    event *dicts*; enforce it at the source.
    """
    p = _event_path(uuid)
    if p is None:
        return
    try:
        if not stat.S_ISREG(os.lstat(p).st_mode):
            return
        with open(p, "rb") as f:
            f.seek(0, os.SEEK_END)
            size = f.tell()
            start = max(0, size - max_bytes)
            f.seek(start)
            if start > 0:
                # The window starts mid-file: the first line is a fragment
                # ONLY when the byte before the window is not a newline.
                # (Eng review: the old code dropped a genuine first line
                # whenever the seek landed exactly on a line boundary.)
                f.seek(start - 1)
                boundary = f.read(1) == b"\n"
                f.seek(start)
            else:
                boundary = True
            data = f.read()
    except OSError:
        return
    lines = data.decode("utf-8", errors="replace").splitlines()
    if not boundary and lines:
        lines = lines[1:]
    for line in lines:
        line = line.strip()
        if not line:
            continue
        try:
            obj = json.loads(line)
        except json.JSONDecodeError:
            continue
        if isinstance(obj, dict):
            yield obj


def _event_scan_truncated(uuid, max_bytes=_EVENT_SCAN_MAX_BYTES):
    """True when the event file exists and exceeds the scan window.

    The scan only sees the tail; anything older is invisible to this pass.
    Loud by design (see _EVENT_SCAN_MAX_BYTES): the caller pages
    events-scan-truncated every pass while this holds.
    """
    p = _event_path(uuid)
    if p is None:
        return False
    try:
        return os.lstat(p).st_size > max_bytes
    except OSError:
        return False


# Issue #27: manager-owned bounded growth for hook event files. Hooks append
# with O_APPEND by name on every event (plugin/hooks/stop.py,
# session-end.py), so a rename-based rotate is atomic w.r.t. concurrent hook
# writes: a hook that opened its fd before the rename lands its line in the
# archive, one that opens after lands in the fresh file -- every non-done
# line ends up in exactly one file, never lost, never duplicated (done
# claims are intentionally present in both: the archive keeps them and the
# fresh file re-seeds them, fail-loud). The threshold sits at 2x the scan
# window so the 2 MB tail-scan containment only ever fires on files the
# rotation hasn't caught up with yet.
_EVENT_ROTATE_BYTES = 2 * _EVENT_SCAN_MAX_BYTES
# Straggler tail-sweep passes after the initial done extraction (see
# rotate_event_file): a hook that opened its fd before the rename but wrote
# while extraction was running lands in the archive after the read cursor.
_ROTATE_TAIL_SWEEPS = 5
# Grace for a hook caught mid-write at the final tail read (a single
# write() lands in microseconds; 50ms is enormous margin).
_ROTATE_TAIL_GRACE_S = 0.05


def _rotate_threshold_label():
    """Human rendering of _EVENT_ROTATE_BYTES for operator-facing text."""
    if _EVENT_ROTATE_BYTES % (1024 * 1024) == 0:
        return "%d MB" % (_EVENT_ROTATE_BYTES // (1024 * 1024))
    return "%d bytes" % _EVENT_ROTATE_BYTES


def _done_lines_of(path, start=0):
    """(done_lines, end_offset): raw byte lines of the file from `start`
    whose parsed JSON is a dict with state == "done", in file order.

    Same predicate as done_events() (state == "done" on a dict). Raw lines
    are preserved byte-identical so the re-seeded claims triage exactly as
    they did pre-rotation. Non-dict / malformed / non-done lines are left
    out -- they live on in the archive.

    Fragment handling: if `start` lands mid-line (a racing hook's partial
    write), the leading fragment is skipped -- only whole lines are ever
    returned. A trailing fragment (no terminating newline: a hook caught
    mid-write) is NOT returned; instead end_offset is backed up to the
    fragment's start so the next tail pass re-reads it whole. end_offset is
    the read cursor at EOF for tail re-scans.
    """
    with open(path, "rb") as f:
        f.seek(0, os.SEEK_END)
        size = f.tell()
        if start > size:
            return [], start
        f.seek(start)
        if start > 0:
            # The byte before the cursor tells whether it sits on a line
            # boundary: anything else means a racing hook's partial line.
            f.seek(start - 1)
            if f.read(1) != b"\n":
                f.readline()  # discard the fragment (or the whole tail)
        chunk_start = f.tell()
        data = f.read()
    if data and not data.endswith(b"\n"):
        # Trailing fragment: back the cursor up so the next tail pass
        # re-reads it whole instead of skipping it as malformed.
        cut = data.rfind(b"\n") + 1
        data = data[:cut]
    end_offset = chunk_start + len(data)
    lines = []
    for raw in data.split(b"\n"):
        if not raw.strip():
            continue
        try:
            obj = json.loads(raw.decode("utf-8", errors="replace"))
        except (ValueError, RecursionError):
            continue
        if isinstance(obj, dict) and obj.get("state") == "done":
            lines.append(raw + b"\n")
    return lines, end_offset


def _reserve_archive_name(p, mtime_ns):
    """Reserve a collision-free archive name via O_EXCL.

    Two rotates in the same mtime tick are infeasible on ns-granularity
    filesystems, but the reservation is cheap and makes the "flooding to
    force a rotate buries nothing" claim hold by construction: a bare
    os.rename would silently overwrite a prior archive.
    """
    candidate = "%s.%d.rot" % (p, mtime_ns)
    counter = 0
    while True:
        try:
            fd = os.open(candidate, os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL | os.O_WRONLY,
                         0o644)
        except FileExistsError:
            counter += 1
            candidate = "%s.%d.%d.rot" % (p, mtime_ns, counter)
            continue
        os.close(fd)
        return candidate


def rotate_event_file(uuid):
    """Rotate an oversized hook event file. Manager-owned (issue #27).

    The trigger is size only, evaluated by the manager (the watch pass calls
    this; the agent's hooks never do). The agent's only lever is appending
    lines, and rotation archives rather than deletes, so flooding to force a
    rotate buries nothing -- and terminal `done` claims are re-seeded into
    the fresh file so a rotated-out genuine done keeps paging every pass
    instead of becoming a silent burial (the #23 B1/B3 class).

    Ordering (rename first, extract from the archive, then write the fresh
    file) keeps every line in exactly one file: a hook appending between the
    rename and the fresh-file write opens by name and lands in the fresh
    file; the done extraction only ever reads the archive, so nothing is
    duplicated except the intentional done re-seed. The fresh file is
    created with O_CREAT (a hook that raced in between created the same
    empty file first) and the re-seed writes use O_APPEND exactly like the
    hooks, so a racing hook append and the re-seed serialize the way hook
    appends serialize among themselves.

    Straggler sweep: a hook that opened its fd BEFORE the rename but wrote
    while the extraction was running lands its line in the archive after the
    read cursor passed. After the re-seed, the archive is tail re-scanned
    from the extraction's end offset until it stops growing (bounded); each
    pass only sees new lines, so no dedup is needed. A hook caught mid-write
    at the final read gets a 50ms grace and one last tail pass. Residual: a
    hook write landing after the final tail read (microsecond window) --
    the same order of rarity as the accepted tail-scan hole.

    Refuses: non-regular event file (symlink/FIFO/device -- tampering, the
    watch pass pages events-tampered), a symlinked EVENTS_DIR (the hooks
    refuse the same way). Fail-closed: any failure after the rename
    restores the archive over the fresh file (rename replaces atomically)
    and returns False with the original file byte-identical -- the
    events-scan-truncated signal keeps firing, which is the honest state.
    Returns True when a rotation happened.
    """
    p = _event_path(uuid)
    if p is None:
        return False
    try:
        if os.path.islink(EVENTS_DIR):
            return False
        st = os.lstat(p)
        if not stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode):
            return False
        if st.st_size <= _EVENT_ROTATE_BYTES:
            return False
        archived = _reserve_archive_name(p, st.st_mtime_ns)
        os.rename(p, archived)
        try:
            done_lines, end = _done_lines_of(archived)
            # O_NOFOLLOW parity with the hooks: a symlinked fresh path
            # fails here instead of writing through it.
            fd = os.open(p, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_APPEND
                         | os.O_NOFOLLOW, 0o644)
            try:
                for line in done_lines:
                    os.write(fd, line)
                for _ in range(_ROTATE_TAIL_SWEEPS):
                    if os.stat(archived).st_size <= end:
                        break
                    more, end = _done_lines_of(archived, start=end)
                    for line in more:
                        os.write(fd, line)
                # A hook caught mid-write leaves the archive ending
                # mid-line; give the single write() 50ms to land and take
                # one final tail pass from the fragment's start.
                with open(archived, "rb") as f:
                    f.seek(-1, os.SEEK_END)
                    mid_line = f.read(1) != b"\n"
                if mid_line:
                    time.sleep(_ROTATE_TAIL_GRACE_S)
                    more, _end = _done_lines_of(archived, start=end)
                    for line in more:
                        os.write(fd, line)
            finally:
                os.close(fd)
        except OSError:
            # The rename already happened: restore the archive over any
            # partial fresh file rather than leave done claims silently
            # dropped (a missing/partial fresh file would also silence
            # the events-scan-truncated signal -- the exact burial class
            # this change exists to eliminate).
            try:
                os.rename(archived, p)
            except OSError:
                pass
            return False
    except OSError:
        return False
    return True


def _maybe_rotate_event_file(uuid, slug, events):
    """Watch-pass hook for issue #27: rotate the job's event file when it
    exceeds _EVENT_ROTATE_BYTES and report it as an `events-rotated`
    signal. Separated from watch_one so the emission contract is pinned
    at runtime (cf. _emit_tui_swap_event): the caller passes a uuid that
    already cleared the events-tampered checks. Returns True when a
    rotation happened."""
    if rotate_event_file(uuid):
        events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "events-rotated",
                       "detail": "hook event file exceeded the %s "
                                 "rotation threshold; archived and "
                                 "re-seeded with its preserved done "
                                 "claims" % _rotate_threshold_label()})
        return True
    return False


def event_file_tampered(uuid):
    """True when the session's event file exists but is not a regular file:
    symlink, FIFO, device, directory (issue #3, final review).

    The same-user agent can plant any of these to blind or hijack the watch
    read (a FIFO additionally hangs a blocking reader). A MISSING file is
    not tampered -- that is events-missing. Checked with lstat so a symlink
    is never followed.
    """
    p = _event_path(uuid)
    if p is None:
        return False
    try:
        return not stat.S_ISREG(os.lstat(p).st_mode)
    except OSError:
        return False


def last_event(uuid):
    ev = None
    for e in _iter_events(uuid):
        ev = e
    return ev


def done_events(uuid, now):
    """All `done`-state events in the file, oldest first by normalized ts.

    Issue #23 B3: the newest-only triage let a newer lazy forgery displace
    a genuine done claim in the same watch window -- the watch pass triaged
    only the forgery (done-unverified) and the genuine done-claimed page
    never fired. Callers iterate every done and triage each one with
    handle_done_claim. Final review: there is no persistent dedup -- every
    visible done pages every pass (fail loud); the operator's `close` ends
    the stream.
    """
    evs = []
    for e in _iter_events(uuid):
        if e.get("state") == "done":
            evs.append((e, _claim_ts(e, now)))
    evs.sort(key=lambda t: t[1])
    return evs


def verify_done_event(job, ev, slug):
    """Triage a hook `done` event: protocol compliance, NOT authenticity.

    Hook events are cooperative telemetry, not ground truth: the job agent
    runs as the same user with a full shell, so a sophisticated agent can
    read this tooling and satisfy every check below (write a SUMMARY.md,
    append a matching event line with the right session_id and cwd). This
    verifies that the agent *followed the done protocol*, not that the work
    happened or that the event is genuine.

    `slug` is the manager-side job slug (argv / directory listing), never
    job["slug"]: the worktree under test is DERIVED from it, because
    job.json is agent-writable (issue #11) and the recorded `worktree`
    field must not steer a security check -- a forged worktree would make
    a forged event's cwd pass containment.

    A `done` event passes triage only when all of:
      1. the event names this job's recorded session uuid,
      2. the event carries a cwd at or under the derived job worktree,
      3. the job dir holds a non-empty SUMMARY.md regular file.
    Returns (True, "") or (False, reason). Fail closed. Callers must NEVER
    transition job state on the result alone -- see handle_done_claim.
    """
    ev = ev or {}
    uuid, _malformed = _session_uuid_ok(job)
    if not uuid:
        return False, "job has no recorded session uuid"
    if ev.get("session_id") != uuid:
        return False, "event session_id does not match the job's session"
    cwd = ev.get("cwd")
    # Issue #23 H4: os.path.realpath(123) raises TypeError, contained
    # per-job but persistent. Fail closed on a non-string cwd.
    if not isinstance(cwd, str) or not cwd:
        return False, "event carries no cwd"
    if not slug_ok(slug or ""):
        # The slug feeds a path join below. Production callers pass the
        # argv/listdir slug; a forged job["slug"] (or a missing one) must
        # fail closed here, never widen the containment check silently.
        return False, "no valid job slug to derive the worktree from"
    work = os.path.join(job_dir(slug), "work")
    rcwd, rwork = os.path.realpath(cwd), os.path.realpath(work)
    if rcwd != rwork and not rcwd.startswith(rwork + os.sep):
        return False, f"event cwd {cwd!r} is outside the job worktree"
    sp = os.path.join(job_dir(slug), "SUMMARY.md")
    try:
        # Must be a real non-empty file: getsize() succeeds on directories,
        # so `mkdir SUMMARY.md` would otherwise satisfy this check.
        summary_ok = os.path.isfile(sp) and os.path.getsize(sp) > 0
    except OSError:
        summary_ok = False
    if not summary_ok:
        return False, "SUMMARY.md missing, empty, or not a file"
    return True, ""


def _claim_ts(ev, now):
    """Normalize a claim's event ts for ordering and the 24h burst window.

    Non-numeric/bool/NaN -> 0; future ts clamped to now. Final review: the
    normalized ts is NOT a dedup cursor -- there is no persistent suppression
    anywhere in agent-writable state (issue #11) -- it only orders claims
    oldest-first (issue #23 B3) and sizes the done-repeated burst window.
    """
    ts = (ev or {}).get("ts")
    if isinstance(ts, bool) or not isinstance(ts, (int, float)) or ts != ts:
        return 0
    return min(ts, now)


def handle_done_claim(job, ev, slug, now=None, recent_count=0):
    """Triage a hook `done` event into an operator page. ALWAYS returns a
    signal dict -- never None.

    Never transitions job state (issue #3): the event is forgeable by the
    same-user job agent, so a `done` line is only ever a *claim* the operator
    must confirm (inspect SUMMARY.md, then `muse-job close`).

    `slug` is threaded through to verify_done_event (manager-side slug, so
    the worktree check is derived, not read from agent-writable job.json).

    `done-claimed` = the agent followed the done protocol;
    `done-unverified` = it failed protocol triage (lazy forgery or a broken
    hook payload). Neither authenticates the event.

    Final security review: there is NO dedup cursor and NO suppression latch
    in agent-writable job.json. A planted `done_claim_ts` equal to a forged
    done's ts used to suppress BOTH the genuine and the forged claim before
    verification ever ran (demonstrated exploit); a planted
    `done_repeated_for_ts` latch suppressed silently in the same way. Both
    are gone. Every visible done event pages every pass -- deliberately
    fail-loud. The natural end of the stream is the operator running
    `muse-job close`, after which watch no longer scans the job. Repeated
    paging during an unacknowledged claim is the correct behavior; silent
    suppression is not. Burst coalescing: when more than 3 done claims from
    the last 24h are visible in the event file, claims are labeled
    `done-repeated` (count derived from the event file via `recent_count`,
    never from job.json) so a claim-bursting agent degrades to one label
    per claim rather than training the operator to ignore the signal.

    Legacy agent-writable fields from earlier designs (done_claim_ts,
    done_page_count, done_page_window_start, done_repeated_paged,
    done_repeated_for_ts) are migrated out on triage so a planted remnant
    can never be mistaken for live state again.
    """
    if now is None:
        now = time.time()
    ev = ev or {}
    ok, reason = verify_done_event(job, ev, slug)
    detail = _clean_text(ev.get("detail", ""))
    if ok:
        label = "done-claimed"
        text = (f"agent claims done (protocol checks passed; "
                f"confirm SUMMARY.md, then close): {detail}")
    else:
        label = "done-unverified"
        text = (f"agent claims done but failed protocol cross-check: "
                f"{reason}; possible forgery: {detail}")
    for k in ("done_claim_ts", "done_page_count", "done_page_window_start",
              "done_repeated_paged", "done_repeated_for_ts"):
        job.pop(k, None)
    if recent_count > 3:
        return {"signal": "done-repeated",
                "detail": f"{recent_count} done claims in 24h; coalesced: {text}"}
    return {"signal": label, "detail": text}


def _num(v, default):
    """Sanitize a numeric read from agent-writable job.json (issue #3).

    Rejects bool, non-numeric types, and NaN -- garbage fails to the default.
    Without this, a single `"started_at": "x"` write makes job_status raise
    TypeError, which watch_one swallows, silencing every signal for that job
    (sec review round 2).
    """
    if isinstance(v, bool) or not isinstance(v, (int, float)) or v != v:
        return default
    return v


def _session_anchor(job, now):
    """Sanitized liveness anchor: the current session's start time, or None.

    job.json is agent-writable (issue #11), so garbage is normalized here --
    and a missing or poisoned anchor is *untrustworthy*, never innocent: fail
    closed (return None) so callers page loudly instead of anchoring grace
    to `now` and staying dark forever (sec/eng/arch review, issue #23).

    Present-None is treated as missing: cmd_spawn writes
    "session_started_at": None on failed uuid discovery, so the anchor
    falls back to started_at in that case.
    """
    v = job.get("session_started_at")
    if v is None:  # absent OR explicitly None (failed discovery writes None)
        v = job.get("started_at")
    if isinstance(v, bool) or not isinstance(v, (int, float)) or v != v:
        return None
    return min(v, now)


def _tmux_session_created(slug):
    """tmux server-side session creation epoch, or None if unreachable.

    Issue #23 (sec review): the job.json anchor is agent-writable, so an
    agent can bump session_started_at to ~now on every pass and keep the
    discovery grace open forever with the same write primitive the uuid
    wipe needs. The tmux session creation time is server-side state the
    agent cannot rewrite through job.json. An agent that kills its own
    session to mint a fresh timestamp trips the loud tmux-dead path, so
    there is no silent bypass.
    """
    p = None
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(
            ["tmux", "display-message", "-p", "-t", f"mjob-{slug}",
             "#{session_created}"],
            stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, text=True)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return None  # tmux not installed: unreachable
    if p.returncode != 0:
        return None
    try:
        return int(p.stdout.strip())
    except (ValueError, AttributeError):
        return None


def _grace_anchor(job, st, now):
    """Anchor for the liveness grace windows: tmux server-side session
    creation time when available, else the sanitized job.json anchor.

    Falls back to _session_anchor only when tmux is unreachable (a brief
    race at most -- the watch path already established tmux is alive).
    Returns None only when NO trustworthy anchor exists; callers page
    loudly on None (fail closed).
    """
    created = st.get("session_created")
    if isinstance(created, bool) or not isinstance(created, (int, float)) \
            or created != created:
        created = None
    if created is not None:
        return min(created, now)
    return _session_anchor(job, now)


def triage_done_events(job, uuid, now, events):
    """Triage every done event in the file, oldest-first, always paging.

    Issue #23 B3: iterate oldest-first so a newer forgery can never displace
    a genuine claim in the same window. Final review: no dedup cursor, no
    suppression latch -- every visible done event produces a signal on every
    pass (fail loud); the operator's `close` ends the stream. The 24h burst
    count comes from the event file, never from agent-writable job.json.
    Appends signal dicts (with `job` filled) to `events` and persists the
    job record once for the legacy cursor/latch key migration.
    """
    slug = job["slug"]
    evs = done_events(uuid, now)
    recent_count = sum(1 for _, ts in evs if 0 <= now - ts <= 24 * 3600)
    for ev, _ts in evs:
        sig = handle_done_claim(job, ev, slug, now, recent_count=recent_count)
        sig["job"] = slug
        events.append(sig)
    if evs:
        save_job(job, slug)


def check_events_liveness(job, st, now):
    """Watchdog liveness (issue #3): detect event-file blinding.

    Recency-based: page when the newest parseable hook event is older than
    the discovery grace (or there are none at all) while tmux is alive and
    a session uuid is recorded. The grace is anchored to the tmux
    server-side session creation time when available -- the agent cannot
    rewrite it through job.json -- with the sanitized job.json anchor
    (`session_started_at`, refreshed when the resume fallback re-discovers
    a new session uuid) as fallback, so a resumed job still gets a fresh
    grace window instead of paging on every pass.

    Honest residuals (documented, not defeated): the event stream itself
    lives where the same-user agent can reach it -- an agent appending
    periodic junk heartbeat lines defeats recency detection. The job.json
    anchor fallback is agent-writable; it is only trusted when tmux is
    unreachable, and a missing/poisoned anchor pages loudly (fail closed).
    This catches deletion and total silence, not an agent actively gaming
    the event file.

    Fail-loud: pages on every watch pass while the condition holds. An
    earlier design deduped via a latch in job.json; that latch was plantable
    by the same-user agent (one write = permanent watch blindness), so it was
    removed (sec review round 2). Repeated paging during active blinding is
    the correct behavior.
    Returns a signal dict or None.
    """
    uuid, malformed = _session_uuid_ok(job)
    if not st["tmux_alive"]:
        return None
    if malformed:
        # Paged loudly as events-tampered by the caller; liveness has
        # nothing trustworthy to assess, so it stays silent here.
        return None
    # The grace anchor is the tmux server-side session creation time when
    # available (the agent cannot rewrite it through job.json); the
    # sanitized job.json anchor is only the fallback. anchor None means no
    # trustworthy anchor exists at all -- page, never go dark (fail closed).
    anchor = _grace_anchor(job, st, now)
    if not uuid:
        # Final review (arch): an absent uuid is legitimate ONLY during
        # initial discovery (cmd_spawn discovers within ~90s). job.json is
        # agent-writable: wiping session_uuid to "" or deleting the key
        # blinded liveness AND done triage with zero signal on any pass.
        # After the discovery grace, an absent uuid on a live tmux session
        # pages loudly instead of staying dark. (A job whose spawn discovery
        # genuinely failed also pages -- correctly: the watchdog is blind to
        # it either way.)
        if anchor is None or now - anchor > _DISCOVERY_GRACE_S:
            detail = ("tmux alive but no session uuid recorded past "
                      "the discovery grace window; the watchdog "
                      "cannot see this job's events (uuid wiped or "
                      "discovery failed)")
            if anchor is None:
                detail += " -- and no trustworthy session anchor exists"
            return {"signal": "events-tampered", "detail": detail}
        return None
    if event_file_tampered(uuid):
        return None  # handled by the dedicated events-tampered signal
    if anchor is None:
        # A recorded session uuid with no trustworthy anchor is anomalous:
        # spawn and resume always set the anchor alongside the uuid. The
        # watchdog cannot bound the recency grace, so page loudly instead
        # of staying dark (anchor poisoned or tmux unreachable + garbage
        # job.json).
        return {"signal": "events-tampered",
                "detail": "tmux alive and session uuid recorded, but no "
                          "trustworthy session anchor exists; cannot bound "
                          "the liveness grace (anchor poisoned?)"}
    le = st["last_hook_event"]
    le_ts = _claim_ts(le, now) if isinstance(le, dict) else 0
    if le_ts >= anchor:
        return None
    if now - anchor <= _DISCOVERY_GRACE_S:
        return None
    return {"signal": "events-missing",
            "detail": "tmux alive and session uuid recorded, but the newest "
                      "hook event is older than 30m (or none exists) -- event "
                      "file deleted or hooks blinded?"}


# Issue #12 L7: `status`/`watch` size the whole job dir, and a job that
# built Rust or npm trees (target/, node_modules/) paid 15s+ per pass.
# Build-output and VCS dirs carry no operator signal, so prune them from
# the walk instead of sizing files the operator never asked about.
_DIR_SIZE_SKIP_DIRS = frozenset(("target", "node_modules", ".git"))


def dir_size(path):
    total = 0
    for root, dirs, files in os.walk(path):
        dirs[:] = [d for d in dirs if d not in _DIR_SIZE_SKIP_DIRS]
        for fn in files:
            try:
                total += os.path.getsize(os.path.join(root, fn))
            except OSError:
                pass
    return total


# Issue #12 item L7 (second half): the prune above cut the worst of it, but
# the walk still ran on EVERY status/watch pass (15s+ per job observed).
# Cache the measured size on the job record and re-walk at most every
# 15 minutes. The only consumer is watch's 20 GB "heavy" signal, which
# tolerates a coarse reading; the cache is a hint, not a boundary (a
# corrupted or non-numeric stored value fails back to a fresh walk via
# _num, never to a crash). The walk itself stays un-cached: job.json is
# agent-writable (#11), so the fresh value is always re-derived from disk.
_DIR_BYTES_TTL_S = 15 * 60


def _cached_dir_bytes(job, slug):
    now = time.time()
    cached = _num(job.get("last_dir_bytes"), None)
    scanned_at = _num(job.get("last_dir_scan_at"), 0)
    if scanned_at > now:
        # A future timestamp is clock skew or job.json tamper (#11): fail
        # to a fresh walk, never serve a cached value past its own clock.
        scanned_at = 0
    if cached is not None and now - scanned_at < _DIR_BYTES_TTL_S:
        return cached
    total = dir_size(job_dir(slug))
    job["last_dir_bytes"] = total
    job["last_dir_scan_at"] = now
    save_job(job, slug)
    return total


def job_status(job, slug):
    alive = tmux_alive(slug)
    uuid, _malformed = _session_uuid_ok(job)
    hstatus, nbytes = head_info(uuid) if uuid else (None, 0)
    delta = nbytes - _num(job.get("last_bytes"), 0)
    if delta:
        job["last_bytes"] = nbytes
        save_job(job, slug)
    ev = last_event(uuid) if uuid else None
    try:
        pm = os.path.getmtime(os.path.join(job_dir(slug), "PROGRESS.md"))
        progress_age = time.time() - pm
    except OSError:
        progress_age = None
    # Binary TUI-liveness probe for the watchdog and status render: tmux
    # alive says nothing about the Muse session inside it (a model-stream
    # death parks the pane at the TUI's resume banner with no hook event).
    # pane_cmd (the pane's foreground process name) is also the input to
    # the TUI-binary-swap detector (issue #212): an in-place Muse
    # auto-update shows up as a change in that name across watchdog passes.
    pane_live, pane_banner = False, False
    pane_cmd = ""
    pane_trust = False
    pane_trust_unverified = False
    if alive:
        pane, pane_cmd = _capture_pane_state(f"mjob-{slug}")
        pane_live = _pane_shows_live_tui(pane, pane_cmd)
        pane_banner = (not pane_live) and _pane_shows_resume_banner(pane, pane_cmd)
        # Issue #791: the TUI is up but parked at the workspace-trust gate
        # (spawn's post-launch answer missed it, or the gate appeared later).
        # Trust text in the tail implies not-live (per _pane_shows_live_tui's
        # own rule); the foreground-process gate is what keeps a dead pane
        # with stale prompt text in its scrollback from counting (issue #4:
        # a dead pane is never sent keystrokes). Issue #961: the gate needs
        # the full shape (question + option line), not the bare question
        # substring, so a live TUI's conversation merely mentioning the
        # phrase does not flip the signal.
        pane_trust = ((not pane_live) and _is_tui_process(pane_cmd)
                      and _pane_shows_trust_gate(pane))
        # Issue #836 (regression of #791): the gate can be showing while
        # the foreground process is NOT a recognized TUI process -- a new
        # binary name after an in-place auto-update (issue #212), a
        # wrapper launcher, or a tmux capture hiccup -- and then
        # pane_trust stays clear while the job strands. The trust TEXT is
        # still in the tail, so this is observably a trust gate, not
        # generic tui-dead -- but issue #4 still forbids keystrokes into a
        # pane whose process identity is unverified, so this reads as a
        # LOUD operator-actionable signal (blocked-trust-unverified),
        # never as an auto-answer attempt.
        pane_trust_unverified = ((not pane_live) and (not pane_trust)
                                 and _pane_shows_trust_gate(pane))
    return {
        "slug": slug,
        "job_state": job.get("state"),
        "tmux_alive": alive,
        "pane_live_tui": pane_live,
        "pane_resume_banner": pane_banner,
        "pane_trust_prompt": pane_trust,
        # Issue #836: trust gate visible but foreground process not a
        # recognized TUI process -- loud blocked-trust-unverified, never
        # auto-answered (issue #4).
        "pane_trust_unverified": pane_trust_unverified,
        "pane_cmd": pane_cmd,
        # Server-side session creation time: the unspoofable anchor for the
        # liveness grace windows (issue #23, sec review). None when tmux is
        # unreachable; _grace_anchor falls back to the job.json anchor then.
        "session_created": _tmux_session_created(slug) if alive else None,
        "session_uuid": uuid,
        "session_status": hstatus,
        "bytes_total": nbytes,
        "bytes_delta": delta,
        "last_hook_event": ev,
        "progress_age_s": progress_age,
        "dir_bytes": _cached_dir_bytes(job, slug),
        "elapsed_h": (time.time() - _num(job.get("started_at"), time.time())) / 3600,
        # Issue #23 B6: budget_hours is agent-writable (job.json); coerce at the
        # source so the terminal render below can never carry escapes.
        "budget_hours": _num(job.get("budget_hours"), 8),
    }


def _safe_hhmm(ts):
    """Render an agent-influenced event ts for display, never raising
    (issue #23 H3): a forged "ts": "x" used to raise TypeError and
    "ts": 1e30 OverflowError in time.localtime, killing `muse-job status`
    persistently. Garbage renders as ??:??."""
    try:
        if isinstance(ts, bool) or not isinstance(ts, (int, float)) or ts != ts:
            return "??:??"
        return time.strftime("%H:%M", time.localtime(ts))
    except (OverflowError, OSError, ValueError):
        return "??:??"


def cmd_status(args):
    job = load_job(args.slug)
    if _msp_transport(job):
        return _msp_status(job, args.slug, args)
    st = job_status(job, args.slug)
    if args.json:
        print(json.dumps(st, indent=2, default=str))
    else:
        ev = st["last_hook_event"] or {}
        # Issue #23 B6: job_state (job.json) and session_status (HEAD.json) are
        # agent-influenced; sanitize before printing to the operator's
        # terminal (\r + escape sequences could repaint the rendered line).
        print(f"job {st['slug']}: state={_clean_text(st['job_state'])} tmux={'up' if st['tmux_alive'] else 'down'} "
              f"session={_clean_text(st['session_status'])} bytes={st['bytes_total']} (+{st['bytes_delta']})")
        if st["tmux_alive"] and not st["pane_live_tui"]:
            # The session=healthy above comes from HEAD.json and can be
            # stale for days after a model-stream death; the pane probe is
            # the ground truth for whether anything can make progress.
            if st["pane_resume_banner"]:
                print("  pane: TUI session exited (resume banner shown) -- "
                      "watchdog will attempt /resume --last")
            else:
                print("  pane: TUI not live (no input box; not a resume banner either) -- "
                      "inspect with `muse-job log`")
        if ev:
            flag = ""
            if ev.get("state") == "done":
                # Issue #3: a `done` line is a claim, never proof -- the
                # same-user agent can forge every check. Label what the
                # triage actually established, nothing more.
                ok, reason = verify_done_event(job, ev, args.slug)
                flag = (" (claims done; protocol checks passed)"
                        if ok else
                        f" (claims done; protocol check failed: {reason})")
            # Issue #23 B6: event/state come from the agent-writable event file
            # (the agent can append lines directly, bypassing hook
            # sanitizers) -- strip terminal escapes the same as detail.
            print(f"  last turn: {_clean_text(ev.get('event'))}/{_clean_text(ev.get('state'))}{flag} "
                  f"{_safe_hhmm(ev.get('ts'))} "
                  f"{_clean_text(ev.get('detail',''))[:100]}")
        if st["progress_age_s"] is not None:
            print(f"  PROGRESS.md age: {st['progress_age_s']/60:.0f}m  elapsed: {st['elapsed_h']:.1f}h / budget {st['budget_hours']}h")
    return 0


def cmd_list(args):
    out = []
    if os.path.isdir(JOBS_DIR):
        for slug in sorted(os.listdir(JOBS_DIR)):
            p = job_json_path(slug)
            if os.path.isfile(p):
                try:
                    job = load_job(slug)
                    out.append({
                        "slug": slug,
                        "state": job.get("state"),
                        "transport": job.get("transport") or "tmux",
                        "tmux_alive": tmux_alive(slug),
                        "session_uuid": job.get("session_uuid"),
                        # Sanitized like job_status (issue #3): a non-numeric
                        # started_at in agent-writable job.json must not make
                        # the job silently vanish from `list`.
                        "elapsed_h": round((time.time() - _num(job.get("started_at"), time.time())) / 3600, 1),
                    })
                except Exception:
                    pass
    if args.json or not out:
        print(json.dumps(out, indent=2))
    else:
        for j in out:
            # Issue #23 B6: state comes from agent-writable job.json -- sanitize.
            print(f"{j['slug']}: {_clean_text(j['state'])} {_clean_text(j.get('transport') or 'tmux')} tmux={'up' if j['tmux_alive'] else 'down'} {j['elapsed_h']}h")
    return 0


def cmd_log(args):
    try:
        job = load_job(args.slug) if os.path.isfile(job_json_path(args.slug)) else None
    except Exception:
        job = None
    if job is not None and _msp_transport(job):
        return _msp_log(args)
    if not tmux_alive(args.slug):
        raise RuntimeError("tmux session not alive")
    n = max(1, args.n)
    pane = run("tmux", "capture-pane", "-p", "-t", f"mjob-{args.slug}").stdout.decode(
        errors="replace")
    lines = pane.splitlines()
    # #12 L3: capture-pane output is agent-influenced; print it through the
    # same ANSI/control-character sanitizer as every other operator-facing
    # surface so a crafted pane cannot inject terminal escape sequences.
    print(_clean_text("\n".join(lines[-n:])))
    return 0


# Issue #12 item L4: grace between SIGTERM and SIGKILL when reaping a
# job's process tree. Short enough to keep the CLI snappy; detached
# grandchildren that ignore SIGTERM get SIGKILL right after.
_KILL_SIGTERM_GRACE_S = 3.0


def _tmux_pane_pids(slug):
    """PIDs of the tmux panes backing the job session (tree roots).

    Issue #12 L4 (Security B2): dead panes are excluded — a pane whose
    process already exited may have had its PID recycled by an unrelated
    process before our snapshot, and the starttime guard only covers
    post-snapshot recycling. Only live pane PIDs may be kill roots.
    """
    try:
        out = run("tmux", "list-panes", "-t", f"mjob-{slug}",
                  "-F", "#{pane_pid} #{pane_dead}", timeout=15).stdout.decode(
                      errors="replace")
    except Exception:
        return []
    pids = []
    for line in out.splitlines():
        parts = line.split()
        # pane_dead == 0 -> live pane; anything else is never a root.
        if len(parts) == 2 and parts[0].isdigit() and parts[1] == "0":
            pids.append(int(parts[0]))
    return pids


def _proc_starttime(pid):
    """Boot-relative start time of a PID, or None if unreadable.

    Field 22 of /proc/<pid>/stat; the comm field (in parens) can contain
    spaces and parens, so fields are counted from the last ')': token[0]
    is field 3 (state), hence starttime is token[19]. (Security B1: an
    earlier draft used token[20] — field 23, vsize — which both changes
    under memory allocation and collides across identical processes,
    breaking the reuse guard in both directions.)
    """
    try:
        with open(f"/proc/{pid}/stat") as f:
            st = f.read()
        return int(st.rsplit(")", 1)[1].split()[19])
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
        return None


def _proc_state(pid):
    """Single-letter process state (field 3 of /proc/<pid>/stat), or None
    if unreadable. Z (zombie) matters to the reaper: a zombie holds no
    resources and cannot be signaled — only its parent's wait() clears it
    — so the tree kill treats it as already dead."""
    try:
        with open(f"/proc/{pid}/stat") as f:
            st = f.read()
        return st.rsplit(")", 1)[1].split()[0]
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
        return None


def _descendant_pids(roots):
    """All PIDs in the process subtrees rooted at `roots`.

    ps-based (no psutil dependency — muse-job is a dependency-free
    script); best-effort, snapshot semantics.
    """
    try:
        out = run("ps", "-eo", "pid,ppid", timeout=15).stdout.decode(
            errors="replace")
    except Exception:
        return set()
    children = {}
    for line in out.splitlines()[1:]:
        parts = line.split()
        if len(parts) == 2 and parts[0].isdigit() and parts[1].isdigit():
            children.setdefault(int(parts[1]), []).append(int(parts[0]))
    seen = set()
    stack = list(roots)
    while stack:
        p = stack.pop()
        if p in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(p)
        stack.extend(children.get(p, ()))
    return seen


def _kill_process_tree(slug):
    """Issue #12 L4: reap the job's whole process tree, not just the
    tmux session.

    `tmux kill-session` kills the pane processes, but detached
    grandchildren (nohup'd, backgrounded builds, setsid'd helpers) are
    reparented away from the pane and survive — runaway jobs persisted
    past kill. So: snapshot the pane subtree first, kill the session,
    then SIGTERM -> (grace) -> SIGKILL the survivors and verify.

    PID-reuse guard: starttimes are snapshotted before any signal, and
    every kill/verify step re-checks /proc/<pid>/stat's starttime — a
    PID that exits and gets recycled after the snapshot is never
    signaled. Two honest residuals: (a) a PID recycled between tmux's
    list-panes and our snapshot (microsecond window; dead panes are
    excluded from roots to shrink it); (b) grandchildren that detached
    (setsid/double-fork) before the kill are invisible to a kill-time
    snapshot and remain unreaped — same as the old behavior, strictly
    safer than signaling a possibly-wrong PID. Only own-UID processes
    are signalable anyway; EPERM is fail-silent (the PID lands in the
    returned leaked list).

    Returns the list of PIDs that could not be reaped (empty on the
    happy path). Never raises: kill must not fail the operator's
    command — the leaked list is the honest signal.
    """
    roots = _tmux_pane_pids(slug)
    tree = _descendant_pids(roots) if roots else set()
    stamped = {}
    for p in tree:
        st = _proc_starttime(p)
        if st is not None:
            stamped[p] = st

    def alive_same(p):
        # Zombies are already dead for reaping purposes (a zombie's
        # /proc entry outlives the process until the parent wait()s;
        # signaling it is a no-op and reporting it as leaked is noise).
        # Unreadable state (None) means the process is gone.
        return (p in stamped and _proc_starttime(p) == stamped[p]
                and _proc_state(p) not in ("Z", None))

    # Security (required): the never-raises guarantee covers a missing
    # tmux binary too — subprocess.run raises FileNotFoundError, and the
    # old bare kill-session had the same hole.
    try:
        run("tmux", "kill-session", "-t", f"mjob-{slug}", check=False)
    except Exception:
        pass
    survivors = [p for p in stamped if alive_same(p)]
    for p in survivors:
        try:
            os.kill(p, signal.SIGTERM)
        except (ProcessLookupError, PermissionError):
            pass
    deadline = time.time() + _KILL_SIGTERM_GRACE_S
    while time.time() < deadline:
        survivors = [p for p in survivors if alive_same(p)]
        if not survivors:
            break
        time.sleep(0.2)
    for p in survivors:
        if not alive_same(p):
            continue
        try:
            os.kill(p, signal.SIGKILL)
        except (ProcessLookupError, PermissionError):
            pass
    time.sleep(0.5)
    leaked = [p for p in survivors if alive_same(p)]
    return leaked


def cmd_kill(args):
    # MSP jobs have no tmux pane or process tree: session/delete + state.
    try:
        job = load_job(args.slug) if os.path.isfile(job_json_path(args.slug)) else None
    except Exception:
        job = None
    if job is not None and _msp_transport(job):
        return _msp_kill(args)
    # Issue #12 L4: kill the session AND reap the pane process trees —
    # detached grandchildren must not survive the kill.
    leaked = _kill_process_tree(args.slug)
    # Engineering review: "tree_killed": True with a non-empty leaked
    # list would be misleading — the honest signal is the leak list.
    out = {"slug": args.slug, "tmux": "killed",
           "tree_killed": not leaked, "leaked_pids": leaked}
    if leaked:
        print("muse-job: warning: %d process(es) survived kill: %s"
              % (len(leaked), ",".join(map(str, leaked))), file=sys.stderr)
    # Issue #5: kill is an explicit operator act and must be terminal-ish.
    # A killed job kept state "active", so the next `watch` pass treated the
    # dead tmux as a crash and auto-resumed it -- the operator's deliberate
    # kill was undone within one cron period. Record "killed": watch only
    # acts on active/blocked jobs, so it leaves a killed job alone, and
    # `resume` is the deliberate re-activation that returns it to "active".
    # Unknown slugs (no job.json) keep the old permissive behavior: kill's
    # tmux/process semantics are unchanged for them.
    if os.path.isfile(job_json_path(args.slug)):
        try:
            job = load_job(args.slug)
            job["state"] = "killed"
            save_job(job, args.slug)
            out["state"] = "killed"
        except Exception as e:
            # QA review: the state mark is advisory, never load-bearing.
            # A corrupt or unreadable job.json is exactly when the operator
            # most needs kill to work -- kill's process/tree semantics must
            # stay never-raises (per _kill_process_tree's contract), so a
            # failed mark warns on stderr and keeps rc=0.
            print(f"muse-job: warning: could not record killed state: {e}",
                  file=sys.stderr)
    print(json.dumps(out))
    return 0


# `muse resume <uuid>` failure modes that mean "the old session is gone;
# start a fresh TUI and continue from git state + prompt.md" instead of
# reporting a successful resume. The TUI's wording varies across versions --
# "no retained sessions found for this workspace" was observed 2026-09-21
# when a model-stream death left no resumable session behind.
_RESUME_GONE_RE = re.compile(
    r"session not found|no session found|could not find session|"
    r"unknown session|no such session|no retained sessions", re.I)


def _answer_trust_prompt(slug, timeout=20):
    """Answer the TUI's workspace-trust prompt if it is showing.

    Called after launching a TUI in the job's own workdir (resume and the
    fallback-fresh path). The workdir is slug-derived and operator-owned --
    the job was spawned there -- so selecting "Trust and continue" grants
    nothing the job's agent doesn't already have through its shell; it only
    unblocks the TUI boot. Without this, resume hangs at the prompt
    forever. Text and Enter are separate send-keys calls, per the tmux
    input rule. Never raises: a missed prompt just leaves the existing
    behavior (the caller proceeds and its own checks decide).
    """
    target = f"mjob-{slug}"
    deadline = time.time() + timeout
    try:
        while time.time() < deadline:
            pane, pane_cmd = _capture_pane_state(target)
            # Security (PR #816 review, issue #791): never send keystrokes on
            # content alone. A dead pane can hold the stale prompt text in
            # its scrollback tail, and typing "1"+Enter into that shell is a
            # false answer (issue #4: a dead pane is never sent keystrokes).
            # The gate fires only while the foreground process is the TUI --
            # the prompt only renders then, so genuine gates still answer.
            # Issue #961: require the gate's full observed shape (question
            # + option line), not the bare question substring -- a live TUI
            # discussing the gate in its own conversation must not be
            # answered into.
            if (_is_tui_process(pane_cmd)
                    and _pane_shows_trust_gate(pane)):
                run("tmux", "send-keys", "-t", target, "1", timeout=30)
                time.sleep(1)
                run("tmux", "send-keys", "-t", target, "Enter", timeout=30)
                return True
            if _pane_shows_live_tui(*_capture_pane_state(target)):
                return False
            time.sleep(2)
    except Exception:
        pass
    return False


def _recover_trust_prompt(slug):
    """Answer a workspace-trust gate the watchdog found a live TUI parked at.

    Issue #791: spawn's post-launch answer can miss the gate (or the gate
    appears after a later relaunch), and the old code lumped the aftermath
    into generic tui-dead while the job stranded. Answer under the same
    standing --yolo authorization as the resume path (the workdir is
    slug-derived and operator-owned, so "Trust and continue" grants nothing
    the job's agent doesn't already have through its shell), then verify the
    pane actually went live. Returns the watch event: `trust-answered` when
    the TUI is live again, `blocked-trust` (loud, operator-actionable) when
    the gate is still showing after the answer attempt. Never raises:
    _answer_trust_prompt is itself no-raise, and a capture failure reads as
    still-blocked (fail closed).
    """
    _answer_trust_prompt(slug)
    try:
        pane, cmd = _capture_pane_state(f"mjob-{slug}")
        live = _pane_shows_live_tui(pane, cmd)
    except Exception:
        live = False
    if live:
        return {"job": slug, "signal": "trust-answered",
                "detail": "TUI was blocked at the workspace-trust prompt; "
                          "answered under the standing --yolo authorization "
                          "and the TUI is live again"}
    return {"job": slug, "signal": "blocked-trust",
            "detail": "TUI still shows the workspace-trust prompt after "
                      "the automatic answer attempt -- operator remedy: "
                      "inspect with muse-job log, answer the prompt manually, "
                      "or muse-job kill <slug> and spawn a fresh job"}


def cmd_resume(args, _from_watch=False):
    job = load_job(args.slug)
    if _msp_transport(job):
        return _msp_resume(args, _from_watch)
    if tmux_alive(args.slug):
        # QA review: a live session means the job is running. A stale
        # "killed" record -- reachable because kill's session-kill runs
        # check=False and can fail silently while the state mark lands --
        # would otherwise leave the job permanently invisible to the
        # watchdog (watch skips non-active/blocked; every resume refuses).
        # Heal the record to the truth, then refuse as before.
        if job.get("state") == "killed":
            job["state"] = "active"
            save_job(job, args.slug)
        raise RuntimeError("tmux session already alive")
    uuid, _malformed = _session_uuid_ok(job)
    if not uuid:
        raise RuntimeError("no session uuid recorded; cannot resume")
    # Re-derive from the slug: job["worktree"] is agent-writable (issue #11)
    # and must not choose where the operator's tmux starts a shell.
    work, _, _ = trusted_job_paths(args.slug, job)
    # Re-validate the uuid->workdir binding: job["session_uuid"] is
    # agent-writable, and `muse resume <uuid>` would otherwise attach the
    # operator's tmux to whatever session a forged uuid names (cross-job
    # steering). Fail closed with an explicit message.
    if not uuid or not session_bound_to_workdir(uuid, work):
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"recorded session uuid {uuid!r} is not bound to this job's "
            f"workdir {work!r} in the hook registry -- possible job.json "
            "tampering; refusing resume")
    run("tmux", "new-session", "-d", "-s", f"mjob-{args.slug}", "-x", "220", "-y", "60",
        "-c", work)
    run("tmux", "send-keys", "-t", f"mjob-{args.slug}",
        f"cd {shquote(work)} && {_tui_launch_cmd('resume', shquote(uuid))}", "Enter")
    _answer_trust_prompt(args.slug)
    time.sleep(15)
    pane = run("tmux", "capture-pane", "-p", "-t", f"mjob-{args.slug}").stdout.decode(
        errors="replace")
    # Only fall back on a genuine "session not found" style message. The
    # previous inline pattern lived inside a broader wording match that
    # normal TUI output (e.g. "0 errors", a filename) could trip --
    # abandoning a good session; the tightened _RESUME_GONE_RE above is
    # restricted to gone-session wordings.
    if _RESUME_GONE_RE.search(pane):
        # Fallback: fresh TUI, continue from git state.
        run("tmux", "send-keys", "-t", f"mjob-{args.slug}", "q")
        time.sleep(2)
        t0 = time.time()
        run("tmux", "send-keys", "-t", f"mjob-{args.slug}",
            f"cd {shquote(work)} && {_tui_launch_cmd('--yolo')}", "Enter")
        _answer_trust_prompt(args.slug)
        time.sleep(8)
        _steer(args.slug, "Continue the job described in ../prompt.md. "
               "Review git state (git log, git status, git diff) and PROGRESS.md, "
               "then carry on.", verify=False)
        # The fallback is a brand-new session with a new uuid. The recorded
        # uuid still points at the dead session -- watch would then read the
        # dead session's event file forever and never see the new session's
        # turn events (issue #3 review). Re-discover it like cmd_spawn does,
        # explicitly excluding the dead uuid: a pure since_ts filter cannot
        # reliably exclude the old registration within its 10s slack.
        old_uuid = job.get("session_uuid")
        uuid = None
        deadline = time.time() + 90
        while time.time() < deadline and uuid is None:
            uuid = find_session(work, t0, exclude=old_uuid)
            if uuid is None:
                time.sleep(5)
        if uuid:
            job["session_uuid"] = uuid
            # Fresh session, fresh liveness grace (issue #3): the new session
            # writes to a new event file, so the 30m events-missing window
            # must restart here, not at the original spawn. Done-claim state
            # is event-file derived (final review), so a fresh session's
            # fresh file starts the stream cleanly; nothing per-job to keep.
            job["session_started_at"] = t0
            save_job(job, args.slug)
        else:
            print("WARNING: new session uuid not discovered; watch keeps "
                  "reading the old session's events", file=sys.stderr)
        print(json.dumps({"slug": args.slug, "resumed": "fallback-fresh"}))
    else:
        print(json.dumps({"slug": args.slug, "resumed": uuid}))
    # Issue #5: a watch pass can be mid-recovery when the operator kills
    # (or closes) the job -- cmd_resume takes minutes (sleeps, pane
    # probes, up to 90s of session re-discovery). In the auto path only,
    # re-read the record before re-activating and refuse to revive a job
    # that left the monitored states: the operator's mid-recovery decision
    # stands. Tear down the session we just created so "killed => no live
    # session" holds. The operator path (_from_watch=False) keeps
    # unconditional activation: resume is the sanctioned undo of a kill,
    # which is why the provenance flag is required.
    if _from_watch:
        fresh = load_job(args.slug)
        if fresh.get("state") not in ("active", "blocked"):
            run("tmux", "kill-session", "-t", f"mjob-{args.slug}", check=False)
            raise RuntimeError(
                f"job {args.slug} left the monitored states during "
                "auto-recovery (operator may have killed or closed it); "
                "tore down the recovery session and left the job alone")
    if job.get("state") != "active":
        job["state"] = "active"
        save_job(job, args.slug)
    return 0


def cmd_close(args):
    def go():
        # Serialized on the per-slug lock: without it a close racing a
        # spawn could delete the job dir / branch out from under the
        # in-flight spawn (same hazard class as the spawn-spawn race).
        _close(args.slug)

    with_lock(args.slug, go)
    return 0


def _close(slug):
    job = load_job(slug)
    if _msp_transport(job):
        # MSP jobs have no pane or process tree: delete the serve session,
        # then run the shared archive/remove-worktree flow below.
        _msp_close_runtime(slug, job)
    else:
        # Issue #12 L4: same tree-reap as cmd_kill — closing a job must not
        # leave detached grandchildren running against the removed worktree.
        _kill_process_tree(slug)
    # Destructive git inputs re-derived from the slug, never from the
    # agent-writable job.json (issue #11): a recorded branch="main" must not
    # reach `git branch -D`, nor a recorded worktree pointing at another
    # job's tree reach `worktree remove --force`.
    work, pristine, branch = trusted_job_paths(slug, job)
    if os.path.isdir(work):
        run("git", "-C", pristine, "worktree", "remove", "--force", work, check=False)
    run("git", "-C", pristine, "worktree", "prune", check=False)
    # Delete the job branch so re-spawning the same slug works
    # (worktree add -b would otherwise fail: branch already exists).
    run("git", "-C", pristine, "branch", "-D", branch, check=False)
    jd = job_dir(slug)
    tmpd = os.path.join(jd, "tmp")
    shutil.rmtree(tmpd, ignore_errors=True)
    for fn in os.listdir(jd):
        if fn.endswith(".log") and not fn.endswith(".gz"):
            p = os.path.join(jd, fn)
            run("gzip", "-f", p, check=False)
    job["state"] = "closed"
    job["closed_at"] = time.time()
    save_job(job, slug)
    print(json.dumps({"slug": slug, "state": "closed"}))
    return 0


def git_diffstat(work):
    try:
        p = run("git", "-C", work, "diff", "--shortstat", timeout=30)
        return p.stdout.decode().strip()
    except Exception:
        return ""


def cmd_watch(args):
    """One watchdog pass over active jobs. Emits JSON lines; never fails."""
    events = []

    def watch_one(slug):
        p = job_json_path(slug)
        if not os.path.isfile(p):
            return
        try:
            job = load_job(slug)
        except Exception:
            return
        if job.get("state") not in ("active", "blocked"):
            return
        if _msp_transport(job):
            # MSP jobs poll the event stream instead of probing a tmux
            # pane; the recovery ladder replaces the resume-banner logic.
            # Never let an MSP exception escape the pass (the tmux path
            # below is wrapped the same way by the caller).
            try:
                _watch_msp_job(slug, job, events, time.time())
            except Exception as e:
                events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "needs-attention",
                               "detail": f"MSP watch pass raised: {e}"})
            return
        st = job_status(job, slug)
        now = time.time()

        if not st["tmux_alive"]:
            # One recovery attempt.
            # Issue #5: the gate above was checked before the (slow)
            # job_status probes -- re-check immediately before recovering.
            # A watch pass already inside watch_one when `kill` (or `close`)
            # lands must not start a multi-minute auto-recovery of a
            # deliberately killed job.
            try:
                fresh = load_job(slug)
            except Exception:
                fresh = None
            if fresh is None or fresh.get("state") not in ("active", "blocked"):
                events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "needs-attention",
                               "detail": "tmux dead and the job left the "
                               "monitored states during this pass (operator "
                               "may have killed or closed it); not "
                               "auto-recovering"})
                return
            # Issue #804: one-phase-job-at-a-time rule (user 2026-10-01).
            # Never auto-resume a phase-* job while another phase-* job has
            # a live tmux session -- this is what resurrected three killed
            # phase jobs into four concurrent ultra sessions on 2026-10-01.
            # Checked after the #5 state gate so a job the operator
            # deliberately killed mid-pass still reports needs-attention
            # (not deferred). Emits `deferred` (same signal and detail the
            # 2026-10-01 operator hot-patch emitted): the job is resumed
            # deliberately (`muse-job resume`) or by a later watch pass
            # once no sibling phase-* job is live.
            if _phase_concurrency_bars_resume(slug):
                events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "deferred",
                               "detail": "tmux dead; auto-resume deferred — another phase-* job is live (one-at-a-time rule)"})
                return
            try:
                cmd_resume(argparse.Namespace(slug=slug), _from_watch=True)
                events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "recovered",
                               "detail": "tmux was dead; resumed session"})
            except Exception as e:
                events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "needs-attention",
                               "detail": f"tmux dead and resume failed: {e}"})
            return

        # Dead-TUI detection: tmux is up but the Muse session inside it has
        # exited (a model-stream death parks the pane at the TUI's resume
        # banner; a crashed TUI drops to a shell). No hook ever fires for
        # this, so without this check the job reads "active/healthy"
        # indefinitely while nothing can make progress. No early return:
        # the hook-telemetry signals below stay independent (fail-loud
        # allows several signals per pass), and the recovery attempt only
        # fires on the narrow resume-banner shape.
        if not st["pane_live_tui"]:
            if st["pane_trust_prompt"]:
                events.append(_recover_trust_prompt(slug))
            elif st["pane_trust_unverified"]:
                # Issue #836: the workspace-trust prompt text is visible in
                # the pane tail but the foreground process is not a
                # recognized TUI process -- the #791 auto-answer cannot run
                # (issue #4: never send keystrokes into an unverified pane),
                # and the old code lumped this into generic tui-dead, which
                # is exactly how the #836 job stranded silently. Loud and
                # actionable instead; the remedy answers the gate by hand.
                events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "blocked-trust-unverified",
                               "detail": "the workspace-trust prompt is showing "
                                         f"but the pane's foreground process "
                                         f"({_clean_text(st['pane_cmd'])!r}) "
                                         "is not the Muse TUI -- refusing to "
                                         "auto-answer (issue #4). Operator "
                                         "remedy: inspect with muse-job log, "
                                         "answer the prompt manually in the "
                                         "pane, or muse-job kill <slug> and "
                                         "resume"})
            elif st["pane_resume_banner"]:
                try:
                    if _recover_resume_banner(slug):
                        events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "tui-recovered",
                                       "detail": "TUI session had exited to the resume banner; "
                                                 "sent /resume --last and the TUI is live again"})
                    else:
                        events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "tui-unrecovered",
                                       "detail": "TUI session exited to the resume banner; "
                                                 "/resume --last did not restore a live TUI "
                                                 "-- operator remedy: muse-job kill <slug>, then "
                                                 "muse-job resume <slug>"})
                except Exception as e:
                    events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "tui-unrecovered",
                                   "detail": f"resume-banner recovery raised: {e}"})
            else:
                events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "tui-dead",
                               "detail": "tmux alive but the TUI is not live and no resume "
                                         "banner is shown -- pane may be a shell or a crashed TUI; "
                                         "refusing to send anything (issue #4). Operator remedy: "
                                         "inspect with muse-job log, then muse-job kill <slug> "
                                         "and muse-job resume <slug>"})

        # Issue #212: detect an in-place Muse auto-update (TUI binary swap)
        # as its own signal instead of lumping the aftermath into tui-dead.
        # Runs after the dead-TUI block so the per-pass liveness probe and
        # any banner recovery already happened this pass. The emission
        # contract (event appended with the job key, baseline persisted)
        # is pinned by test_watch_emission_contract in
        # tests/test_tui_update_signal.py.
        _emit_tui_swap_event(job, slug, st["pane_cmd"], events)

        if not job.get("session_uuid"):
            # Spawn's 90s uuid discovery can miss a slow first model call.
            # Without a uuid the job is half-blind (no liveness, done-claim,
            # or tamper signals) and un-resumable, forever. Adopt it lazily
            # when the hook registration appears. The workdir is re-derived
            # from the slug, not read from agent-writable job.json.
            found, attention = maybe_adopt_session(job, slug)
            if found:
                job["session_uuid"] = found
                job["session_started_at"] = time.time()
                save_job(job, slug)
                events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "session-adopted",
                               "detail": "late session uuid discovery; "
                                         "liveness monitoring now active"})
            elif attention:
                # Fail loud on every pass while the condition holds (same
                # policy as events-tampered): a plantable latch in job.json
                # would let the agent silence this permanently.
                events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "needs-attention",
                               "detail": attention})

        uuid, uuid_malformed = _session_uuid_ok(job)

        # Issue #3, final review: a non-regular event file (symlink, FIFO,
        # device, directory) is tampering, not silence -- page it on every
        # pass while the condition holds (fail loud). A present-but-malformed
        # session_uuid is the same class of tamper: it must not silently
        # blind liveness and done triage. An earlier design deduped via a
        # latch in job.json; the latch was plantable by the same-user agent
        # (one write = permanent watch blindness), so it was removed
        # (sec review round 2).
        liv = None
        if uuid_malformed:
            liv = {"signal": "events-tampered",
                   "detail": "recorded session_uuid is malformed (not a "
                             "hook-shaped id); refusing event reads"}
        elif uuid and event_file_tampered(uuid):
            liv = {"signal": "events-tampered",
                   "detail": "hook event file is not a regular file "
                             "(symlink, FIFO, device, or directory); "
                             "refusing to read it"}
        else:
            # Issue #3 liveness: an agent that deletes its event file blinds
            # the watchdog with no signal at all -- page it, every pass while
            # blind (same plantable-latch reasoning as above).
            liv = check_events_liveness(job, st, now)
        if liv is not None:
            liv["job"] = slug
            events.append(liv)

        # Issue #27: manager-owned bounded growth, before the truncated
        # signal so a freshly rotated file doesn't page both signals in one
        # pass. Rotation is size-triggered by the manager only -- the agent
        # cannot force it, and terminal done claims are re-seeded into the
        # fresh file so nothing genuine is buried. The tampered lstat is
        # computed once and shared with the truncated gate below.
        if uuid:
            tampered = event_file_tampered(uuid)
            if not tampered:
                _maybe_rotate_event_file(uuid, slug, events)

            # Final review: the 2 MB scan window is containment, not
            # correctness. A genuine event pushed past the tail is invisible
            # to the scan, so truncation is a LOUD per-pass signal, never a
            # silent condition.
            if not tampered and _event_scan_truncated(uuid):
                events.append({
                    "job": slug, "signal": "events-scan-truncated",
                    "detail": "hook event file exceeds the 2 MB watch scan "
                              "window; done claims outside the window are "
                              "invisible to this pass -- investigate manually"})

        ev = st["last_hook_event"] or {}
        estate = ev.get("state")
        if estate == "blocked" and job.get("state") != "blocked":
            job["state"] = "blocked"
            save_job(job, slug)
            events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "blocked",
                           "detail": _clean_text(ev.get("detail", ""))})
        elif estate == "question":
            events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "question",
                           "detail": _clean_text(ev.get("detail", ""))})

        # Issue #23 B1/B2/B3, final review: a hook `done` is only ever a
        # claim -- the same-user agent can forge every check -- so it never
        # transitions job state and there is no persistent dedup cursor or
        # suppression latch in agent-writable job.json (both were
        # demonstrably plantable). triage_done_events triages EVERY done
        # event oldest-first on every pass (a newer forgery can never
        # displace a genuine claim); repeated paging of an unacknowledged
        # claim is the correct fail-loud behavior, ended by `muse-job close`.
        if uuid:
            triage_done_events(job, uuid, now, events)

        if st["elapsed_h"] > (_num(job.get("budget_hours"), 8) or 8):
            events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "budget-exceeded",
                           "detail": f"{st['elapsed_h']:.1f}h > {_num(job.get('budget_hours'), 8)}h budget (notify only)"})

        # Thrash heuristic: bytes growing, no progress heartbeat, no diff change.
        # The worktree is re-derived from the slug: job["worktree"] is
        # agent-writable (issue #11) and must not choose what we diff.
        diffstat = git_diffstat(os.path.join(job_dir(slug), "work"))
        if diffstat == job.get("last_diffstat") and diffstat:
            job["diffstat_strikes"] = _num(job.get("diffstat_strikes"), 0) + 1
        else:
            job["diffstat_strikes"] = 0
            job["last_diffstat"] = diffstat
        save_job(job, slug)
        pa = st["progress_age_s"]
        if (st["bytes_delta"] > 0 and pa is not None and pa > 90 * 60
                and job["diffstat_strikes"] >= 3):
            events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "possible-thrash",
                           "detail": "bytes growing, PROGRESS.md stale >90m, diff unchanged 3 passes"})
        if st["bytes_delta"] == 0 and pa is not None and pa > 45 * 60 \
                and estate not in ("blocked", "done"):
            # idle but session healthy: only flag if tmux pane also looks idle
            events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "idle-unexplained",
                           "detail": "no byte growth for 45m+ with no blocked/done marker"})
        if st["dir_bytes"] > 20 * 1024**3:
            events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "heavy",
                           "detail": f"job dir {st['dir_bytes']/1024**3:.1f}GB"})

    if not os.path.isdir(JOBS_DIR):
        return 0
    for slug in sorted(os.listdir(JOBS_DIR)):
        # The per-job body is read-modify-write on job.json (state
        # transitions, byte counters, diffstat strikes) and a pass can take
        # 15s+; serialize overlapping runs with the job lock, like steer.
        try:
            with_lock(slug, lambda: watch_one(slug))
        except Exception:
            pass

    for e in events:
        print(json.dumps(e), flush=True)
    return 0


# === MSP transport (issue #227: muse-job v2) ==============================
#
# muse-job v2 drives jobs over the Muse Session Protocol (MSP) -- an NDJSON
# JSON-RPC 2.0 stream to `muse serve` -- instead of scraping a TUI inside a
# tmux pane. MSP is the default transport; `--tmux` opts back into the
# legacy TUI-pane path. A job records its transport in job.json
# ("transport": "msp" | "tmux", absent = tmux for legacy records) and
# every subcommand branches on the record.
#
# Module map (all live beside this script, deployed together per the README
# deploy map): msp_host (transport+handshake), msp_session (session
# lifecycle), msp_turn (turn steering), msp_events (notification -> job
# state view), msp_recovery (dead-turn recovery ladder), msp_approval
# (blocking prompts and approvals, issue #225).
#
# Security: the MSP event stream can carry real secrets in tool results and
# transcripts. Nothing in this section prints or logs transcript text --
# operator surfaces (status/log/watch) render only the redacted JobSignal
# stream (structural facts, never free text). The serve child's stderr is
# logged to a 0o600 file inside the job dir (msp_host's doing).

_MSP_IMPORTS = None


def _msp_imports():
    """Import the msp_* sibling modules next to this script.

    Raises RuntimeError with a clear message when the modules were not
    deployed alongside muse-job (the README deploy map lists them)."""
    global _MSP_IMPORTS
    if _MSP_IMPORTS is None:
        here = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
        if here not in sys.path:
            sys.path.insert(0, here)
        try:
            import msp_host
            import msp_session
            import msp_turn
            import msp_events
            import msp_recovery
        except ImportError as e:
            raise RuntimeError(
                "MSP transport needs msp_host/session/turn/events/recovery.py "
                f"deployed beside muse-job: {e}")
        _MSP_IMPORTS = (msp_host, msp_session, msp_turn, msp_events,
                        msp_recovery)
    return _MSP_IMPORTS


def _msp_transport(job):
    """True when the job record says MSP. Absent transport = tmux (legacy)."""
    return (job.get("transport") or "tmux") == "msp"


def _msp_require_sid(job, slug):
    sid = job.get("session_uuid")
    if not sid:
        raise RuntimeError(f"job {slug}: no MSP session id recorded; "
                           "cannot drive this job")
    return sid


def _msp_host_for(slug):
    """Open an MSPHost on `muse serve` for one job. Caller must close().

    Issue #212: MUSE_NO_AUTO_UPDATE=1 -- the serve host must not self-update
    mid-job; an in-place binary swap would desync the protocol mid-turn."""
    msp_host, _, _, _, _ = _msp_imports()
    os.environ["MUSE_NO_AUTO_UPDATE"] = "1"
    host = msp_host.MSPHost(
        ["muse", "serve"],
        client_name="muse_job",
        log_path=os.path.join(job_dir(slug), "tmp", "msp-serve.log"),
    )
    host.open()
    return host


def _msp_call(slug, job, fn, pre_resume=True):
    """Run fn(host) against a fresh serve connection; always close it.

    A fresh `muse serve` host loads no sessions, so the job's recorded
    session is resumed first -- without this, view/subscribe and turn
    calls fail with "session is not loaded on this host". Resume on a
    fresh host is idempotent (nothing is loaded yet). pre_resume=False
    skips the resume for callers that manage the session lifecycle
    themselves (the resume command's gone-session fallback).
    """
    _, msp_session, _, _, _ = _msp_imports()
    host = _msp_host_for(slug)
    try:
        if pre_resume:
            sid = _msp_require_sid(job, slug)
            msp_session.resume_session(host, sid)
        return fn(host)
    except Exception as e:
        # Issue #1129: a live detached supervisor holds the session
        # exclusively; serve answers our resume with -32021 "already in
        # use". Translate only when a supervisor is actually alive -- a
        # -32021 with no live supervisor is a foreign holder and stays an
        # error. Callers route around the held session (snapshot / queue).
        if _msp_session_held_error(e) and _supervisor_alive(slug):
            raise _MSPSessionHeldElsewhere(
                f"session {job.get('session_uuid')} is held by the live "
                f"detached supervisor for {slug!r}; the supervisor owns "
                f"this turn") from e
        raise
    finally:
        try:
            host.close()
        except Exception:
            pass


# The redacted signal journal: every poll journals its replayed JobSignals
# (kind/method/detail -- no transcript text) as JSON lines. `muse-job log`
# renders the tail. Cap: rewrite when over _MSP_JOURNAL_CAP lines.
_MSP_JOURNAL_CAP = 2000


def _msp_journal_path(slug):
    return os.path.join(job_dir(slug), "msp_signals.jsonl")


def _msp_journal_append(slug, signals):
    """Append JobSignals to the job's redacted journal. Never raises."""
    try:
        path = _msp_journal_path(slug)
        lines = []
        for sig in signals:
            try:
                lines.append(json.dumps({
                    "t": time.time(),
                    "kind": sig.kind,
                    "method": sig.method,
                    "detail": sig.detail,
                }) + "\n")
            except Exception:
                continue
        if not lines:
            return
        with open(path, "a") as f:
            f.writelines(lines)
        # Cap: count cheaply, rewrite keeping the tail.
        try:
            with open(path) as f:
                n = sum(1 for _ in f)
            if n > _MSP_JOURNAL_CAP:
                with open(path) as f:
                    tail = f.readlines()[-_MSP_JOURNAL_CAP:]
                tmp = path + ".tmp"
                with open(tmp, "w") as f:
                    f.writelines(tail)
                os.replace(tmp, path)
        except OSError:
            pass
    except Exception:
        pass


def _msp_poll(host, slug, job, replay_wait=2.0):
    """One poll_session_view with journaling; returns the JobView."""
    _, _, _, msp_events, _ = _msp_imports()
    sid = _msp_require_sid(job, slug)
    seen = []

    def sink(sig):
        seen.append(sig)

    view = msp_events.poll_session_view(
        host, sid, job_dir(slug), replay_wait=replay_wait, sink=sink)
    _msp_journal_append(slug, seen)
    return view


# -- detached per-turn serve-host supervisor (issue #1129) -------------------
# `_spawn_msp` used to close its serve host at the end of the spawn, and a
# turn cannot outlive its accepting host (2026-10-04/06 diagnosis): every
# MSP spawn killed its own first turn. The supervisor is a detached
# `muse-job _msp_supervise <slug>` process that holds ONE serve host for
# ONE turn until the turn reaches a terminal state. A command that starts
# a turn closes its own transient host, launches the supervisor, and waits
# for its first heartbeat -- by the time the command returns, the turn is
# held by the supervisor and no host close can kill it. While a supervisor
# holds the session, serve answers resume with -32021 "already in use";
# `_msp_call` translates that (only when a supervisor is actually alive)
# into `_MSPSessionHeldElsewhere`, and each live-job command routes around
# it: status reads the supervisor's view snapshot, steer appends to the
# flock-guarded queue, watch reads the snapshot (the recovery ladder
# cannot attach while the supervisor holds the session), resume reports
# supervisor-alive, kill/close SIGTERM the supervisor first.

_SUPERVISOR_HEARTBEAT_TTL_S = 120
_SUPERVISOR_POLL_INTERVAL_S = 10
_SUPERVISOR_MAX_CONSECUTIVE_POLL_FAILURES = 5
_SUPERVISOR_RESUME_RETRY_S = 30
_SUPERVISOR_BOOT_WAIT_S = 60
_SUPERVISOR_CLAIM_TTL_S = 90
_SUPERVISOR_TERMINATE_WAIT_S = 10
_SUPERVISOR_PID_FILE = "supervisor.pid"
_SUPERVISOR_CLAIM_FILE = "supervisor.claim"
_SUPERVISOR_HEARTBEAT_FILE = "supervisor.heartbeat"
_SUPERVISOR_VIEW_FILE = "supervisor.view.json"
_SUPERVISOR_STEER_QUEUE = "steer-queue.jsonl"
_SUPERVISOR_LOG_FILE = "supervisor.log"


class _MSPSessionHeldElsewhere(Exception):
    """The job's session is held by a live detached supervisor (-32021)."""


def _supervisor_paths(slug):
    jd = job_dir(slug)
    return {
        "pid": os.path.join(jd, _SUPERVISOR_PID_FILE),
        "claim": os.path.join(jd, _SUPERVISOR_CLAIM_FILE),
        "heartbeat": os.path.join(jd, _SUPERVISOR_HEARTBEAT_FILE),
        "view": os.path.join(jd, _SUPERVISOR_VIEW_FILE),
        "queue": os.path.join(jd, _SUPERVISOR_STEER_QUEUE),
        "log": os.path.join(jd, _SUPERVISOR_LOG_FILE),
    }


def _supervisor_cmdline_matches(paths, pid, slug):
    """True when /proc says `pid` is our `_msp_supervise` for this slug.

    The pid file lives in the agent-writable job dir (issue #11): a planted
    pid must never redirect a SIGTERM at an arbitrary process. The cmdline
    check is the guard; the heartbeat gate in `_supervisor_alive` is the
    second one.
    """
    try:
        with open(f"/proc/{pid}/cmdline", "rb") as f:
            cmdline = f.read().replace(b"\0", b" ").decode("utf-8",
                                                           "replace")
    except OSError:
        return False
    return ("_msp_supervise" in cmdline and "muse-job" in cmdline
            and slug in cmdline.split())


def _supervisor_pid_alive(paths, slug):
    """True when the pid file names a live process that looks like our supervisor."""
    try:
        with open(paths["pid"]) as f:
            pid = int(f.read().strip().split()[0])
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
        return False
    if pid <= 0:
        return False
    if os.path.isdir(f"/proc/{pid}"):
        return _supervisor_cmdline_matches(paths, pid, slug)
    # No /proc entry: either the process is gone or /proc is unavailable
    # (non-Linux). Distinguish via kill-0; the heartbeat gate below still
    # bounds spoofing to a same-user forger (issue #3's trust model).
    try:
        os.kill(pid, 0)
    except OSError:
        return False
    return True


def _supervisor_heartbeat_fresh(paths):
    try:
        return (time.time() - os.path.getmtime(paths["heartbeat"])
                <= _SUPERVISOR_HEARTBEAT_TTL_S)
    except OSError:
        return False


def _supervisor_alive(slug):
    """True when a supervisor for this slug is live and attached (or booting).

    Liveness = the pid file names a live supervisor process AND (the
    heartbeat is fresh OR the launch claim is younger than
    `_SUPERVISOR_CLAIM_TTL_S` -- the claim covers the boot window before
    the first heartbeat lands).
    """
    paths = _supervisor_paths(slug)
    if not _supervisor_pid_alive(paths, slug):
        return False
    if _supervisor_heartbeat_fresh(paths):
        return True
    try:
        return (time.time() - os.path.getmtime(paths["claim"])
                <= _SUPERVISOR_CLAIM_TTL_S)
    except OSError:
        return False


def _supervisor_log(paths, msg):
    """Append a line to the supervisor log. Never raises."""
    try:
        with open(paths["log"], "a") as f:
            f.write(f"{time.strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S')} {msg}\n")
    except OSError:
        pass


def _supervisor_beat(paths):
    try:
        with open(paths["heartbeat"], "w") as f:
            f.write(f"{time.time():.3f}\n")
    except OSError:
        pass


def _supervisor_write_snapshot(paths, view):
    snap = {
        "state": view.state,
        "active_turn_id": view.active_turn_id,
        "pending_summary": view.pending_summary,
        "last_event_at": view.last_event_at,
        "last_terminal": getattr(view, "last_terminal", None),
        "snapshot_at": time.time(),
    }
    tmp = paths["view"] + ".tmp"
    try:
        with open(tmp, "w") as f:
            json.dump(snap, f)
        os.replace(tmp, paths["view"])
    except OSError:
        pass


def _supervisor_read_snapshot(slug):
    """Read the supervisor's view snapshot; None when absent/malformed."""
    try:
        with open(_supervisor_paths(slug)["view"]) as f:
            snap = json.load(f)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None
    if not isinstance(snap, dict) or "state" not in snap:
        return None
    return snap


class _SnapshotView:
    """JobView-shaped shim over a supervisor snapshot (watch/status path).

    The snapshot is supervisor-written (manager trust); like HEAD.json it
    is agent-influenced only through what the turn did, and status output
    already treats such sources as untrusted for terminal rendering.
    """

    def __init__(self, snap):
        self.state = snap.get("state")
        self.active_turn_id = snap.get("active_turn_id")
        self.pending_summary = snap.get("pending_summary")
        self.last_terminal = snap.get("last_terminal")
        self.last_event_at = snap.get("last_event_at")


def _supervisor_enqueue_steer(slug, message, turn_id=None):
    """Append a steer request to the supervisor's queue (flock-guarded).

    The queue file is agent-writable (issue #11); entries are
    shape-validated here and re-validated by the supervisor on drain.
    """
    paths = _supervisor_paths(slug)
    if not isinstance(message, str) or not message:
        raise RuntimeError("refusing to queue an empty/non-string steer message")
    if turn_id is not None and not isinstance(turn_id, str):
        raise RuntimeError("refusing to queue a steer with a non-string turn_id")
    line = json.dumps({"turn_id": turn_id, "message": message,
                       "queued_at": time.time()}) + "\n"
    with open(paths["queue"], "a") as f:
        fcntl.flock(f, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
        try:
            f.write(line)
        finally:
            fcntl.flock(f, fcntl.LOCK_UN)


def _supervisor_drain_queue(paths, host, sid, msp_turn, view):
    """Deliver queued steer entries against the live turn.

    Returns the number delivered. Semantics:
    - turn_id matching the live turn -> steer_turn on it.
    - turn_id None -> steer the live turn if any, else start a fresh turn
      (covers entries queued during the boot window, before any snapshot).
    - turn_id set but not the live turn -> dropped loudly, never steered
      (the turn-id match is the anti-cross-turn guard).
    Malformed entries and delivery failures are dropped loudly, never
    retried: a poison entry must not wedge the supervisor, and a failed
    delivery is visible in supervisor.log for the operator to re-issue.
    """
    try:
        with open(paths["queue"], "a+") as f:
            fcntl.flock(f, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
            try:
                f.seek(0)
                lines = f.readlines()
                f.seek(0)
                f.truncate(0)
            finally:
                fcntl.flock(f, fcntl.LOCK_UN)
    except OSError:
        return 0
    delivered = 0
    for line in lines:
        try:
            entry = json.loads(line)
        except ValueError:
            _supervisor_log(paths,
                            f"dropping malformed steer-queue entry: {line[:120]!r}")
            continue
        if (not isinstance(entry, dict)
                or not isinstance(entry.get("message"), str)
                or not entry["message"]):
            _supervisor_log(paths,
                            f"dropping malformed steer-queue entry: {line[:120]!r}")
            continue
        turn_id = entry.get("turn_id")
        if turn_id is not None and not isinstance(turn_id, str):
            _supervisor_log(paths,
                            f"dropping malformed steer-queue entry: {line[:120]!r}")
            continue
        live = view.active_turn_id
        try:
            if turn_id is not None and turn_id != live:
                _supervisor_log(
                    paths,
                    f"dropping stale steer entry for turn {turn_id!r} "
                    f"(live: {live!r})")
                continue
            if live:
                msp_turn.steer_turn(host, sid, entry["message"],
                                    expected_turn_id=live)
                _supervisor_log(paths, f"steered live turn {live!r} from queue")
            else:
                turn, _result = msp_turn.start_turn(host, sid,
                                                   entry["message"])
                new_id = (turn or {}).get("turnId")
                _supervisor_log(paths,
                                f"started turn {new_id!r} from queued steer")
                view.active_turn_id = new_id
            delivered += 1
        except Exception as e:
            _supervisor_log(paths, f"steer-queue delivery failed: {e}")
    return delivered


def _supervisor_resume_with_retry(host, msp_session, sid, paths):
    """Resume the session on the supervisor's fresh host.

    The spawner closed its own host before launching, so attach is normally
    free; a racing short-lived command can transiently hold it (-32021) --
    retry through that, but never wait out a real holder.
    """
    deadline = time.time() + _SUPERVISOR_RESUME_RETRY_S
    while True:
        try:
            return msp_session.resume_session(host, sid)
        except Exception as e:
            if not _msp_session_held_error(e) or time.time() >= deadline:
                raise
            _supervisor_log(paths, f"resume raced a transient holder; retrying: {e}")
            time.sleep(1.0)


def _msp_session_held_error(e):
    """True when the error is serve's 'session already in use' (-32021).

    Only the -32021 code counts: the message text also matches unrelated
    errors (e.g. "address already in use"), which must not misroute into
    the held path. A held session arriving without the code fails closed
    as a loud error instead of misrouting.
    """
    return getattr(e, "code", None) == -32021


def _sleep_interruptible(secs, stop):
    end = time.time() + secs
    while not stop["flag"] and time.time() < end:
        time.sleep(0.5)


def cmd__msp_supervise(args):
    """Hidden subcommand: the detached per-turn serve-host supervisor.

    Holds ONE `muse serve` host for ONE turn until the turn reaches a
    terminal state: polls the view (heartbeat + snapshot + journal), drains
    the steer queue, exits on terminal turn / stop signal / five consecutive
    poll failures. The finally ALWAYS closes the host, so no orphaned serve
    process survives the supervisor.
    """
    slug = args.slug
    job = load_job(slug)
    sid = _msp_require_sid(job, slug)
    _, msp_session, msp_turn, msp_events, _ = _msp_imports()
    paths = _supervisor_paths(slug)
    try:
        with open(paths["pid"], "w") as f:
            f.write(str(os.getpid()))
    except OSError as e:
        print(f"muse-job: supervisor cannot write pid file: {e}",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    stop = {"flag": False}

    def _on_stop(signum, frame):
        stop["flag"] = True

    signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, _on_stop)
    signal.signal(signal.SIGINT, _on_stop)
    terminal_states = {msp_events.STATE_IDLE, msp_events.STATE_TURN_FAILED,
                       msp_events.STATE_TURN_CANCELLED,
                       msp_events.STATE_SESSION_CLOSED}
    _supervisor_log(paths, f"supervisor starting for turn of session {sid[:8]}...")
    host = _msp_host_for(slug)
    exit_code = 0
    try:
        host.open()
        try:
            _supervisor_resume_with_retry(host, msp_session, sid, paths)
        except Exception as e:
            _supervisor_log(paths, f"resume failed; supervisor exiting: {e}")
            return 3
        failures = 0
        while not stop["flag"]:
            try:
                view = _msp_poll(host, slug, job)
            except Exception as e:
                failures += 1
                _supervisor_log(
                    paths,
                    f"poll failed ({failures}/"
                    f"{_SUPERVISOR_MAX_CONSECUTIVE_POLL_FAILURES}): {e}")
                if failures >= _SUPERVISOR_MAX_CONSECUTIVE_POLL_FAILURES:
                    # Dead-host livelock guard: a serve host that never
                    # answers again must not pin the session forever.
                    _supervisor_log(paths, "poll-failure budget exhausted; "
                                           "releasing the session")
                    exit_code = 2
                    break
                _sleep_interruptible(_SUPERVISOR_POLL_INTERVAL_S, stop)
                continue
            failures = 0
            _supervisor_beat(paths)
            _supervisor_write_snapshot(paths, view)
            if _supervisor_drain_queue(paths, host, sid, msp_turn, view):
                # A drained entry may have started a fresh turn -- re-poll
                # before testing terminality.
                continue
            if view.active_turn_id is None and view.state in terminal_states:
                _supervisor_log(paths,
                                f"turn terminal (state={view.state}); exiting")
                break
            _sleep_interruptible(_SUPERVISOR_POLL_INTERVAL_S, stop)
    finally:
        # The stop flag and every exit path land here: the serve child is
        # always reaped, never orphaned.
        try:
            host.close()
        except Exception:
            pass
        try:
            os.unlink(paths["pid"])
        except OSError:
            pass
        _supervisor_log(paths, f"supervisor exiting (rc={exit_code})")
    return exit_code


def _supervisor_launch(slug):
    """Launch the detached supervisor; wait for its first heartbeat.

    Detached: new session, stdio severed (own log file), cwd /tmp.
    `~/.local/bin` is prepended to the child's PATH (the 2026-10-06 deploy
    lesson: the detached supervisor must not depend on the parent's
    environment). MUSE_NO_AUTO_UPDATE=1 is inherited from this process
    (set by `_msp_host_for`). Returns the supervisor pid.
    """
    paths = _supervisor_paths(slug)
    try:
        os.unlink(paths["pid"])
    except OSError:
        pass
    env = dict(os.environ)
    # Set on the child's env only -- the launcher must not mutate the
    # parent's environment as a side effect.
    env["MUSE_NO_AUTO_UPDATE"] = "1"
    local_bin = os.path.expanduser("~/.local/bin")
    env["PATH"] = local_bin + os.pathsep + env.get("PATH", "")
    with open(paths["claim"], "w") as f:
        f.write(f"{time.time():.3f}\n")
    script = os.path.abspath(__file__)
    logf = open(paths["log"], "a")
    try:
        p = subprocess.Popen(
            [sys.executable, script, "_msp_supervise", slug],
            stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=logf, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,
            cwd="/tmp", start_new_session=True, close_fds=True, env=env)
    finally:
        logf.close()
    deadline = time.time() + _SUPERVISOR_BOOT_WAIT_S
    while time.time() < deadline:
        if _supervisor_heartbeat_fresh(paths):
            return p.pid
        if p.poll() is not None:
            raise RuntimeError(
                f"supervisor for {slug!r} exited during boot "
                f"(rc={p.returncode}); see {paths['log']}")
        time.sleep(0.5)
    raise RuntimeError(
        f"supervisor for {slug!r} did not heartbeat within "
        f"{_SUPERVISOR_BOOT_WAIT_S}s; see {paths['log']}")


def _supervisor_ensure(slug, job):
    """Launch the supervisor unless one is already live; return its pid.

    Call after starting a turn on a transient host -- the supervisor takes
    over the steady-state hold. Records `supervisor_pid` in job.json.
    """
    if _supervisor_alive(slug):
        return job.get("supervisor_pid")
    pid = _supervisor_launch(slug)
    job["supervisor_pid"] = pid
    save_job(job, slug)
    return pid


def _supervisor_terminate(slug):
    """SIGTERM (then SIGKILL) the supervisor. Best-effort, never raises.

    Only signals a pid whose /proc cmdline proves it is our
    `_msp_supervise` for this slug -- a planted pid file can never redirect
    the signal at an arbitrary process (issue #11).
    """
    paths = _supervisor_paths(slug)
    pid = None
    try:
        with open(paths["pid"]) as f:
            pid = int(f.read().strip().split()[0])
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
        pid = None
    if pid and pid > 0 and _supervisor_cmdline_matches(paths, pid, slug):
        try:
            os.kill(pid, signal.SIGTERM)
        except OSError:
            pass
        deadline = time.time() + _SUPERVISOR_TERMINATE_WAIT_S
        while time.time() < deadline:
            try:
                os.kill(pid, 0)
            except OSError:
                break
            time.sleep(0.2)
        else:
            # Re-verify the cmdline before escalating: the pid may have been
            # recycled during the wait; never SIGKILL a process we haven't
            # just verified is still our supervisor.
            if _supervisor_cmdline_matches(paths, pid, slug):
                try:
                    os.kill(pid, signal.SIGKILL)
                except OSError:
                    pass
    for key in ("pid", "claim"):
        try:
            os.unlink(paths[key])
        except OSError:
            pass


def _spawn_msp(slug, args):
    """Spawn a job driven over MSP: worktree setup (shared), then
    session/start + turn/start on `muse serve`."""
    job, work, jd = _spawn_prepare(slug, args, PREAMBLE_MSP, None)
    job["transport"] = "msp"
    save_job(job, slug)
    msp_host, msp_session, msp_turn, msp_events, _ = _msp_imports()
    host = _msp_host_for(slug)
    try:
        session, _cursor = msp_session.start_session(
            host, work, approval_mode=msp_session.YOLO_APPROVAL_MODE)
        sid = session.get("sessionId") or session.get("id")
        if not sid:
            raise RuntimeError(f"session/start returned no session id: "
                               f"{str(session)[:200]}")
        with open(os.path.join(jd, "prompt.md")) as f:
            full_prompt = f.read()
        # Subscribe BEFORE turn/start: the reader thread dispatches
        # only to registered subscribers, so a terminal the server
        # emits in the gap between turn/start returning and a later
        # subscribe would be silently dropped -- and the #994
        # cancellation landed ~1ms after turn start. Buffered notes are
        # replayed for the turn id once the await binds it.
        watch = msp_turn.begin_first_turn_watch(host, sid)
        try:
            turn, _result = msp_turn.start_turn(host, sid, full_prompt)
        except BaseException:
            msp_turn.close_first_turn_watch(watch)
            raise
        # Issue #994: a turn/start that returns is not proof the spawn
        # engaged -- the real serve host cancelled the first turn
        # server-side within ~1ms, and the job sat "active" forever with
        # nothing working. Verify the first turn is alive (or done)
        # before declaring the spawn a success; a dead first turn fails
        # the spawn loudly and marks the job blocked, never active.
        eng = msp_turn.await_first_turn_engagement(watch, turn["turnId"])
        if eng["status"] == "dead":
            # The terminal string is server-controlled: never persist
            # or print it raw (log injection / unbounded input). The
            # raw value stays on the in-memory eng record and the
            # exception attribute for debugging; every emission
            # boundary uses the vocabulary-gated label.
            term_label = msp_turn._terminal_label(eng["terminal"])
            job["session_uuid"] = sid
            job["state"] = "blocked"
            job["stillborn"] = {
                "turn_id": turn["turnId"],
                "terminal": term_label,
                "journal": eng["journal"],
            }
            save_job(job, slug)
            raise msp_turn.TurnStillbornError(
                f"muse-job spawn {slug!r}: the first turn died before "
                f"engaging (terminal={term_label!r}, "
                f"{len(eng['journal'])} events in {eng['elapsed_s']:.1f}s); "
                f"the job is marked blocked, not active. Event methods: "
                f"{','.join(eng['journal'])}. The blocked job record "
                f"(event journal under job.json 'stillborn') is kept at "
                f"{jd} for diagnosis. To retry, pick one path -- the job "
                f"dir cannot be both removed and closed (job.json lives "
                f"inside it, so removing the dir deletes the record "
                f"`close` would archive): new slug (keeps the diagnosis): "
                f"`muse-job spawn <new-slug> --tmux`, then "
                f"`muse-job close {slug}` to archive the blocked record; "
                f"same slug: `muse-job close {slug}` first (this tears "
                f"down the git worktree and deletes the job branch), then "
                f"remove {jd} -- `spawn` refuses a slug whose dir still "
                f"exists -- then `muse-job spawn {slug} --tmux`. If the "
                f"turn was cancelled server-side within ~1s of start, "
                f"this matches issue #994 (session/branchChanged race "
                f"suspect).",
                turn_id=turn["turnId"], terminal=eng["terminal"],
                journal=eng["journal"])
        job["session_uuid"] = sid
        job["session_started_at"] = time.time()
        save_job(job, slug)
    except BaseException:
        # Mirror the tmux path: don't leave a half-spawned job behind.
        try:
            host.close()
        except Exception:
            pass
        raise
    # Issue #1129: the spawn's own host must be closed BEFORE the supervisor
    # attaches -- sessions are single-attach, and a turn cannot outlive its
    # accepting host. The supervisor then holds the turn; the spawn returns
    # only once the first heartbeat proves the handoff.
    host.close()
    try:
        supervisor_pid = _supervisor_ensure(slug, job)
    except Exception:
        # The turn died with the spawn's host and no supervisor holds it:
        # fail loud and mark blocked, never active.
        job["state"] = "blocked"
        job["supervisor_error"] = ("the detached supervisor failed to take "
                                   "over the turn; the turn died with the "
                                   "spawn host")
        save_job(job, slug)
        raise
    print(json.dumps({"slug": slug, "transport": "msp",
                      "session_id": job["session_uuid"], "state": "active",
                      "supervisor_pid": supervisor_pid}))
    return 0


def _msp_steer(job, args):
    """Steer an MSP job: poll the view, then steer the live turn or start a
    fresh one when nothing is running (the turn-id match in the view is the
    anti-cross-turn guard -- a steer never lands on the wrong turn)."""
    slug = args.slug
    _, _, msp_turn, _, _ = _msp_imports()

    def go(host):
        view = _msp_poll(host, slug, job)
        sid = _msp_require_sid(job, slug)
        if view.active_turn_id:
            msp_turn.steer_turn(host, sid, args.message,
                                expected_turn_id=view.active_turn_id)
            return {"steered_turn": view.active_turn_id}
        turn, _result = msp_turn.start_turn(host, sid, args.message)
        return {"started_turn": (turn or {}).get("turnId")}
    try:
        out = _msp_call(slug, job, go)
    except _MSPSessionHeldElsewhere:
        # The supervisor holds the live turn: queue the steer; the
        # supervisor drains the queue against the live turn (turn-id
        # tagged, never cross-turn).
        snap = _supervisor_read_snapshot(slug)
        turn_id = snap.get("active_turn_id") if snap else None
        _supervisor_enqueue_steer(slug, args.message, turn_id)
        out = {"steered": "queued", "turn_id": turn_id}
        print(json.dumps({"slug": slug, **out}))
        return 0
    if out.get("started_turn"):
        # A fresh turn on a transient host dies with the host close --
        # hand it to the supervisor (issue #1129).
        _supervisor_ensure(slug, job)
    print(json.dumps({"slug": slug, **out}))
    return 0


# JobView.state -> the job.json state vocabulary (active/blocked), shared
# by status, log, and the watch pass.
_MSP_STATE_TO_JOB = {
    "working": "active",
    "idle": "active",
    "stalled": "active",      # watchdog runs the recovery ladder
    "turn_cancelled": "active",
    "blocked_approval": "blocked",
    "blocked_input": "blocked",
    "turn_failed": "blocked",  # needs the operator; recovery gave up
    "session_closed": "blocked",
}


def _msp_view_summary(view):
    return {
        "msp_state": view.state,
        "active_turn_id": view.active_turn_id,
        "pending": view.pending_summary,
        "last_activity_age_s": (
            round(time.time() - view.last_event_at, 1)
            if view.last_event_at else None),
    }


def _msp_status_from_supervisor(job, slug):
    """Build the status dict from the supervisor's view snapshot.

    Used when the supervisor holds the session and `_msp_call` cannot
    attach. The snapshot is supervisor-written; fields are rendered
    through the same `_clean_text` gates as live views.
    """
    snap = _supervisor_read_snapshot(slug)
    if snap is None:
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"session is held by a live supervisor for {slug!r} but no view "
            f"snapshot exists yet -- the supervisor may still be booting; "
            f"retry in a few seconds")
    job_state = _MSP_STATE_TO_JOB.get(snap.get("state"), "active")
    if job.get("state") != job_state:
        job["state"] = job_state
        save_job(job, slug)
    paths = _supervisor_paths(slug)
    try:
        hb_age = round(time.time() - os.path.getmtime(paths["heartbeat"]), 1)
    except OSError:
        hb_age = None
    try:
        pm = os.path.getmtime(os.path.join(job_dir(slug), "PROGRESS.md"))
        progress_age_s = time.time() - pm
    except OSError:
        progress_age_s = None
    return {
        "slug": slug,
        "transport": "msp",
        "job_state": job_state,
        "session_uuid": job.get("session_uuid"),
        "elapsed_h": (time.time() - _num(job.get("started_at"),
                                         time.time())) / 3600,
        "budget_hours": _num(job.get("budget_hours"), 8),
        "dir_bytes": _cached_dir_bytes(job, slug),
        "msp_state": snap.get("state"),
        "active_turn_id": snap.get("active_turn_id"),
        "pending": snap.get("pending_summary"),
        "last_activity_age_s": (
            round(time.time() - snap["last_event_at"], 1)
            if snap.get("last_event_at") else None),
        "supervisor_held": True,
        "supervisor_heartbeat_age_s": hb_age,
        "snapshot_age_s": round(time.time() - snap.get("snapshot_at",
                                                       time.time()), 1),
        "progress_age_s": progress_age_s,
    }


def _msp_status(job, slug, args):
    """status for an MSP job: poll the event stream, fold to a JobView."""
    def go(host):
        view = _msp_poll(host, slug, job)
        job_state = _MSP_STATE_TO_JOB.get(view.state, "active")
        if job.get("state") != job_state:
            job["state"] = job_state
            save_job(job, slug)
        st = {
            "slug": slug,
            "transport": "msp",
            "job_state": job_state,
            "session_uuid": job.get("session_uuid"),
            "elapsed_h": (time.time() - _num(job.get("started_at"),
                                             time.time())) / 3600,
            "budget_hours": _num(job.get("budget_hours"), 8),
            "dir_bytes": _cached_dir_bytes(job, slug),
            **_msp_view_summary(view),
        }
        try:
            pm = os.path.getmtime(os.path.join(job_dir(slug), "PROGRESS.md"))
            st["progress_age_s"] = time.time() - pm
        except OSError:
            st["progress_age_s"] = None
        return st
    try:
        st = _msp_call(slug, job, go)
    except _MSPSessionHeldElsewhere:
        # Issue #1129: the supervisor holds the session; report from its
        # view snapshot instead of failing the status.
        st = _msp_status_from_supervisor(job, slug)
    if args.json:
        print(json.dumps(st, indent=2, default=str))
    else:
        held = " supervisor=holding" if st.get("supervisor_held") else ""
        print(f"job {st['slug']}: transport=msp state={_clean_text(st['job_state'])} "
              f"msp={_clean_text(st['msp_state'])} "
              f"turn={_clean_text(st['active_turn_id'] or '-')}{held}")
        if st["pending"]:
            print(f"  pending: {_clean_text(st['pending'])[:200]}")
        if st["progress_age_s"] is not None:
            print(f"  PROGRESS.md age: {st['progress_age_s']/60:.0f}m  "
                  f"elapsed: {st['elapsed_h']:.1f}h / budget {st['budget_hours']}h")
    return 0


def _msp_log(args):
    """log for an MSP job: the redacted signal journal (never transcript
    text -- the event stream can carry real secrets)."""
    slug = args.slug
    n = max(1, args.n)
    path = _msp_journal_path(slug)
    try:
        with open(path) as f:
            lines = f.readlines()
    except OSError:
        raise RuntimeError(f"no MSP signal journal for job {slug}")
    for line in lines[-n:]:
        try:
            e = json.loads(line)
        except ValueError:
            continue
        ts = _safe_hhmm(e.get("t"))
        print(f"{ts} {_clean_text(e.get('kind',''))} "
              f"{_clean_text(e.get('method',''))} "
              f"{_clean_text(e.get('detail',''))[:160]}")
    return 0


def _msp_delete_session_best_effort(host, sid):
    """session/delete with a UUIDv7 idempotency key. Best-effort: teardown
    must never fail the operator's kill/close/resume. The completion
    arrives separately (SS3.24); a refusal here is advisory."""
    _, msp_session, _, _, _ = _msp_imports()
    try:
        host.call("session/delete", {
            "commandId": msp_session.new_command_id(),
            "sessionId": sid,
        })
    except Exception:
        pass


def _msp_kill(args):
    """kill for an MSP job: delete the serve session, mark killed.

    No tmux pane or process tree exists for MSP jobs -- the serve child is
    a per-command connection (opened/closed around each call), so there is
    nothing to reap. The killed state keeps the watchdog off, same as the
    tmux path (issue #5)."""
    slug = args.slug
    job = load_job(slug)
    # Issue #1129: a live supervisor holds the session exclusively -- kill
    # it first so session/delete below can attach.
    _supervisor_terminate(slug)
    sid = job.get("session_uuid")
    if sid:
        def go(host):
            _msp_delete_session_best_effort(host, sid)
        try:
            _msp_call(slug, job, go)
        except Exception as e:
            print(f"muse-job: warning: session/delete failed: {e}",
                  file=sys.stderr)
    job["state"] = "killed"
    save_job(job, slug)
    print(json.dumps({"slug": slug, "transport": "msp", "session": "deleted",
                      "state": "killed"}))
    return 0


def _msp_resume(args, _from_watch=False):
    """resume for an MSP job: session/resume the recorded session id.

    A gone session starts fresh on the same worktree with a re-anchor
    turn (PROGRESS.md tail + do-not-repeat, mirroring the tmux fallback's
    contract). _from_watch keeps the issue-#5 re-read: a job that left the
    monitored states mid-recovery is left alone."""
    slug = args.slug
    job = load_job(slug)
    sid = _msp_require_sid(job, slug)
    _, msp_session, msp_turn, _, _ = _msp_imports()

    def go(host):
        try:
            result = msp_session.resume_session(host, sid)
        except Exception as e:
            if not _msp_session_gone(e):
                raise
            # Fresh session, same worktree: re-anchor like the tmux
            # fallback-fresh path.
            work, _, _ = trusted_job_paths(slug, job)
            session, _cursor = msp_session.start_session(
                host, work, approval_mode=msp_session.YOLO_APPROVAL_MODE)
            new_sid = session.get("sessionId") or session.get("id")
            if not new_sid:
                raise RuntimeError("session/start returned no session id")
            prompt = _reanchor_prompt(slug, job)
            msp_turn.start_turn(host, new_sid, prompt)
            return {"resumed": "fallback-fresh", "session_id": new_sid}
        session = result.get("session", result) if isinstance(result, dict) else {}
        return {"resumed": sid,
                "status": (session or {}).get("status")}
    try:
        out = _msp_call(slug, job, go, pre_resume=False)
    except _MSPSessionHeldElsewhere:
        # Issue #1129: the supervisor holds the session, so the job is
        # alive by definition -- report, don't resume.
        paths = _supervisor_paths(slug)
        try:
            hb_age = round(time.time()
                           - os.path.getmtime(paths["heartbeat"]), 1)
        except OSError:
            hb_age = None
        out = {"resumed": "supervisor-alive",
               "supervisor_pid": job.get("supervisor_pid"),
               "supervisor_heartbeat_age_s": hb_age}
        print(json.dumps({"slug": slug, "transport": "msp", **out}))
        return 0
    if out.get("resumed") == "fallback-fresh":
        job["session_uuid"] = out["session_id"]
        job["session_started_at"] = time.time()
        # The fresh turn is on a transient host -- hand it to the supervisor
        # (issue #1129).
        _supervisor_ensure(slug, job)
    if _from_watch:
        fresh = load_job(slug)
        if fresh.get("state") not in ("active", "blocked"):
            raise RuntimeError(
                f"job {slug} left the monitored states during auto-recovery; "
                "left the job alone")
    if job.get("state") != "active":
        job["state"] = "active"
    save_job(job, slug)
    print(json.dumps({"slug": slug, "transport": "msp", **out}))
    return 0


def _msp_session_gone(err):
    """True when a resume failure means 'no such session' (not a transport
    blip): tightened wording match, mirroring _RESUME_GONE_RE."""
    return bool(_RESUME_GONE_RE.search(str(err)))


def _reanchor_prompt(slug, job):
    """Re-anchor prompt for a fresh MSP session on an existing worktree:
    PROGRESS.md tail + do-not-repeat the completed work (the recovery
    ladder's contract, issue #226)."""
    tail = ""
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(job_dir(slug), "PROGRESS.md")) as f:
            tail = f.read()[-4000:]
    except OSError:
        pass
    return (
        "You are resuming a job whose previous session is gone. "
        "Do NOT repeat work already recorded below; continue from git state "
        "(git log, git status, git diff) and carry on.\n\n"
        "--- PROGRESS.md tail ---\n" + tail)


def _msp_close_runtime(slug, job):
    """Best-effort session/delete for close. Called from _close before the
    shared archive/remove-worktree flow."""
    # Issue #1129: kill the supervisor before session/delete so the close
    # path can attach -- a supervisor left running would also keep its
    # serve child alive past the worktree removal.
    _supervisor_terminate(slug)
    sid = job.get("session_uuid")
    if not sid:
        return
    try:
        def go(host):
            _msp_delete_session_best_effort(host, sid)
        _msp_call(slug, job, go)
    except Exception as e:
        print(f"muse-job: warning: session/delete failed during close: {e}",
              file=sys.stderr)


def _watch_msp_job(slug, job, events, now):
    """One watchdog pass for an MSP job. Emits JSON findings; never raises
    past this boundary (the caller wraps in try/except like the tmux path).

    - blocked_approval/blocked_input -> blocked finding (operator decision)
    - turn_failed -> needs-attention (recovery already gave up)
    - stalled/dead turn -> the msp_recovery ladder (continued/resumed =
      recovered; give_up = needs-attention)
    - turn completed with SUMMARY.md -> done finding (operator confirms,
      then closes)
    """
    stillborn = job.get("stillborn")
    if isinstance(stillborn, dict):
        # Issue #994: the first turn died at spawn before the agent
        # engaged. The job is blocked, not active -- and it must STAY
        # blocked. Without this short-circuit the poll below replays the
        # cancelled turn, maps turn_cancelled -> "active", and the
        # watchdog resurrects the zombie on its next pass.
        term = stillborn.get("terminal")
        label = term if term in ("cancelled", "interrupted", "failed",
                                 "completed", "unknown") else "unknown"
        # Issue #1029: the advice must mirror the two-path retry the
        # TurnStillbornError and the README stillborn-spawn section pin.
        # A bare "retry spawn with --tmux" is actively wrong on the same
        # slug: _spawn_prepare refuses a slug whose job dir still exists,
        # so the operator needs the close-first (and dir removal) path.
        events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "needs-attention",
                       "detail": f"first turn stillborn at spawn "
                                 f"(terminal={label!r}); to retry, pick one "
                                 f"path -- the job dir cannot be both removed "
                                 f"and closed (job.json lives inside it, so "
                                 f"removing the dir deletes the record "
                                 f"`close` would archive): new slug (keeps "
                                 f"the diagnosis): `muse-job spawn "
                                 f"<new-slug> --tmux`, then `muse-job close "
                                 f"{slug}` to archive the blocked record; "
                                 f"same slug: `muse-job close {slug}` first "
                                 f"(tears down the git worktree and deletes "
                                 f"the job branch), remove the job dir, then "
                                 f"`muse-job spawn {slug} --tmux` (spawn "
                                 f"refuses an existing dir)"})
        return
    _, _, _, msp_events, msp_recovery = _msp_imports()
    sid = job.get("session_uuid")
    if not sid:
        events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "needs-attention",
                       "detail": "MSP job with no recorded session id; "
                                 "cannot monitor it"})
        return

    def go(host):
        return _msp_poll(host, slug, job)
    held = False
    try:
        view = _msp_call(slug, job, go)
    except _MSPSessionHeldElsewhere:
        # Issue #1129: the supervisor holds the session, so the recovery
        # ladder below cannot attach. Read the supervisor's snapshot and
        # skip recovery -- a held session is being worked, not dead.
        snap = _supervisor_read_snapshot(slug)
        if snap is None:
            events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "needs-attention",
                           "detail": "MSP poll failed: session held by a "
                                     "live supervisor, no view snapshot yet"})
            return
        view = _SnapshotView(snap)
        held = True
    except Exception as e:
        events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "needs-attention",
                       "detail": f"MSP poll failed: {e}"})
        return

    state = view.state
    job_state = _MSP_STATE_TO_JOB.get(state, "active")
    if job.get("state") != job_state:
        job["state"] = job_state
        save_job(job, slug)

    if state in ("blocked_approval", "blocked_input"):
        events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "blocked",
                       "detail": f"MSP session blocked: "
                                 f"{_clean_text(view.pending_summary)[:200]}"})
        return
    if state == "session_closed":
        events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "needs-attention",
                       "detail": "MSP session closed unexpectedly; "
                                 "`muse-job resume` to recover"})
        return
    if state == "turn_failed":
        events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "needs-attention",
                       "detail": "MSP turn failed; `muse-job steer` to "
                                 "re-anchor or `muse-job kill` to end it"})
        return
    if state == "stalled":
        if held:
            # The supervisor owns this turn; the ladder cannot attach while
            # it holds the session. Surface, don't recover.
            events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "needs-attention",
                           "detail": "MSP turn stalled; session held by the "
                                     "live supervisor -- no recovery "
                                     "attempted (the supervisor owns the "
                                     "turn)"})
            return
        def go2(host):
            # recover_dead_turn polls the view and detects the dead shape
            # internally; it takes (host, session_id, job_dir) and returns
            # a RecoveryReport (never a diagnosis tuple).
            return msp_recovery.recover_dead_turn(host, sid, job_dir(slug))
        try:
            report = _msp_call(slug, job, go2)
        except Exception as e:
            events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "needs-attention",
                           "detail": f"MSP recovery raised: {e}"})
            return
        action = report.action
        note = (report.detail or "; ".join(report.attempts) or "")
        detail = _clean_text(note)[:200]
        if action == msp_recovery.ACTION_NONE:
            return
        if action in (msp_recovery.ACTION_CONTINUED,
                      msp_recovery.ACTION_RESUMED):
            # The revived turn would die with the recovery host's close --
            # hand it to the supervisor (issue #1129).
            try:
                _supervisor_ensure(slug, job)
            except Exception as e:
                events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "needs-attention",
                               "detail": f"MSP stalled turn recovered via "
                                         f"{action} but the supervisor "
                                         f"handoff failed: {e}"})
                return
            events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "recovered",
                           "detail": f"MSP stalled turn recovered via "
                                     f"{action}: {detail}"})
        else:
            events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "needs-attention",
                           "detail": f"MSP stalled turn; recovery {action}: "
                                     f"{detail}"})
        return
    # turn completed -> done claim when the agent wrote SUMMARY.md.
    if view.last_terminal == "completed":
        try:
            summary = os.path.join(job_dir(slug), "SUMMARY.md")
            if os.path.getsize(summary) > 0:
                events.append({"job": slug, "signal": "done",
                               "detail": "MSP turn completed and SUMMARY.md "
                                         "is present; confirm, then "
                                         "`muse-job close`"})
        except OSError:
            pass


def main():
    # --version before argparse: subparsers are required, so argparse alone
    # would reject a bare --version. Only the bare flag counts, so a steer
    # message containing "--version" is never hijacked.
    if sys.argv[1:] in (["--version"], ["-V"]):
        print(SPARKVM_VERSION)
        return 0
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="muse-job")
    sub = ap.add_subparsers(dest="cmd", required=True)

    s = sub.add_parser("spawn", help="start a job")
    s.add_argument("slug")
    s.add_argument("--repo", required=True)
    s.add_argument("--prompt-file", required=True)
    s.add_argument("--base", default=None)
    s.add_argument("--budget-hours", type=float, default=8)
    s.add_argument("--allow-secrets", action="store_true",
                   help="confirm the prompt may carry real secrets; without "
                   "this the issue-#9 scan refuses secret-shaped prompt text")
    s.add_argument("--tmux", action="store_true",
                   help="drive the job in a tmux TUI pane (legacy transport); "
                   "default is MSP over `muse serve` "
                   "(issue #228: muse-job v2)")
    s.set_defaults(fn=cmd_spawn)

    s = sub.add_parser("steer", help="send a message into a running job")
    s.add_argument("slug")
    s.add_argument("message")
    s.add_argument("--allow-secrets", action="store_true",
                   help="confirm the message may carry real secrets; without "
                   "this the issue-#9 scan refuses secret-shaped steer text")
    s.set_defaults(fn=cmd_steer)

    s = sub.add_parser("status", help="inspect one job")
    s.add_argument("slug")
    s.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    s.set_defaults(fn=cmd_status)

    s = sub.add_parser("list", help="all jobs")
    s.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    s.set_defaults(fn=cmd_list)

    s = sub.add_parser("log", help="recent terminal output")
    s.add_argument("slug")
    s.add_argument("-n", type=int, default=60)
    s.set_defaults(fn=cmd_log)

    s = sub.add_parser("kill", help="kill the tmux session and reap its process tree; marks the job 'killed' (watchdog won't revive it)")
    s.add_argument("slug")
    s.set_defaults(fn=cmd_kill)

    s = sub.add_parser("resume", help="recover a dead tmux session")
    s.add_argument("slug")
    s.set_defaults(fn=cmd_resume)

    s = sub.add_parser("close", help="archive the job, remove its worktree")
    s.add_argument("slug")
    s.set_defaults(fn=cmd_close)

    s = sub.add_parser("watch", help="watchdog pass over active jobs")
    s.set_defaults(fn=cmd_watch)

    # Internal: the detached per-turn serve-host supervisor (issue #1129).
    # Launched by _supervisor_launch, never by hand.
    s = sub.add_parser("_msp_supervise",
                       help="internal: detached per-turn MSP supervisor "
                            "(launched by spawn/steer; do not run by hand)")
    s.add_argument("slug")
    s.set_defaults(fn=cmd__msp_supervise)

    args = ap.parse_args()
    try:
        # Issue #7: central slug gate. Only cmd_spawn validated the slug;
        # status/steer/log/kill/resume/close passed the raw argv slug into
        # job_dir() path joins (`muse-job close ..` resolved to ~, then the
        # close path rmtree'd/gzipped outside ~/muse-jobs). Validate once at
        # the boundary so no subcommand can drift out again. cmd_spawn keeps
        # its own check as defense in depth for direct callers; cmd_watch's
        # listdir-derived slugs cannot yield `.`/`..` (listdir never does).
        slug = getattr(args, "slug", None)
        if slug is not None and not slug_ok(slug):
            raise RuntimeError(f"bad slug: {slug}")
        return args.fn(args)
    except RuntimeError as e:
        print(f"muse-job: error: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
